window.AIBRIEFS=window.AIBRIEFS||{};Object.assign(window.AIBRIEFS,{"1988/87-5259":{"facts":"Teague, a black man, was tried in an Illinois court for attempted murder, armed robbery and aggravated battery. The prosecutor used all 10 of his peremptory challenges to strike black venire members, producing an all-white jury. Defense counsel twice moved for a mistrial, saying Teague was entitled to a jury of his peers, and the prosecutor said he was seeking a balance of men and women. The trial court denied the motions and Teague was convicted.","procedure":"The Illinois Appellate Court rejected Teague's fair cross section claim, and review in Illinois and the Supreme Court was denied. In a federal habeas petition he repeated that claim and argued that opinions in McCray v. New York undermined Swain v. Alabama. The district court denied relief; the en banc Seventh Circuit held he could not benefit from Batson v. Kentucky, that his Swain claim was procedurally barred, and that the fair cross section requirement covers only the venire.","question":"May a state prisoner on federal habeas corpus obtain the benefit of Batson, press an equal protection claim under Swain that he never raised in state court, or invoke a new rule extending the Sixth Amendment fair cross section requirement to the petit jury, and under what standard are new rules of criminal procedure applied on collateral review?","holding":"Allen v. Hardy bars use of Batson because Teague's conviction became final before Batson, and his Swain claim is procedurally barred under Wainwright v. Sykes. A plurality adopted Justice Harlan's approach: new constitutional rules of criminal procedure generally do not apply to cases on collateral review unless they fall within one of two narrow exceptions, and neither applies to the petit-jury fair cross section rule, so the Court would not announce it.","reasoning":"On the first two points, the Court said a denial of certiorari carries no precedential weight, so the McCray opinions did not destroy Swain, and Teague forfeited the Swain claim by not raising it at trial or on direct appeal and showed no cause for the default. Justice O'Connor's plurality treated retroactivity as a threshold question because a rule applied to the defendant in the case announcing it must be applied evenhandedly to similarly situated defendants. The older Linkletter standard had produced inconsistent and unequal results, while Griffith v. Kentucky had already adopted Harlan's rule for cases on direct review. Habeas corpus is a collateral remedy, not a substitute for appeal, and interests of comity and finality, along with the burden on States of retrying defendants whose trials met the standards then in force, weigh against applying new rules to final convictions. Harlan's exceptions are rules placing private conduct beyond the power to criminalize and watershed rules, which the plurality limited to procedures without which the likelihood of an accurate conviction is seriously diminished. A petit-jury cross section rule is not such a “bedrock procedural element.” The plurality added that habeas “cannot be used as a vehicle to create new constitutional rules of criminal procedure” that would not be applied retroactively.","separate":"Justice White concurred in the judgment and in Parts I to III: he still disagreed with the direct-review retroactivity cases but accepted the result as consistent with them. Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment as to the Swain claim and joined Part I of Justice Stevens's opinion. Justice Stevens, joined in Part I by Justice Blackmun, concurred in part and in the judgment: he thought the Sixth Amendment claim valid and the Court should decide it, objected to treating retroactivity as a threshold question and to narrowing the fundamental fairness exception to accuracy, but felt bound by Allen v. Hardy, and he would have treated the Swain claim as unexhausted. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented: the plurality adopted a sweeping new limit on habeas without briefing or argument and contrary to precedent and stare decisis, it would bar many constitutional claims, and Teague's claim was a modest extension of Taylor, Duren and Batson.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed. Justice O'Connor announced the judgment and wrote the opinion of the Court as to Parts I, II and III, and for a plurality (with the Chief Justice and Justices Scalia and Kennedy) as to Parts IV and V; Justices Brennan and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep489/usrep489288/usrep489288.pdf"},"1983/82-472":{"facts":"Russello owned a building in Tampa and arranged for arsonists, members of an arson ring, to burn part of it so he could collect insurance. The fire spread, and he received four payments from the insurer totaling $340,043.09, helped by a ring member who served as adjuster and was paid $30,000 out of the proceeds.","procedure":"Russello was convicted in federal district court of RICO racketeering and conspiracy under 18 U.S.C. section 1962(c) and (d), and the jury returned special verdicts forfeiting the insurance payments under section 1963(a)(1). A Fifth Circuit panel reversed the forfeiture, but the en banc court affirmed it by a vote of 16 to 7, in conflict with the Ninth Circuit's Marubeni decision, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do the profits and proceeds of racketeering count as an “interest” acquired in violation of section 1962 that is subject to forfeiture under section 1963(a)(1), or does that provision reach only interests in an enterprise?","holding":"Profits and proceeds of racketeering are an “interest” within section 1963(a)(1) and are subject to forfeiture. The provision is not limited to interests in an enterprise.","reasoning":"Justice Blackmun began with the statutory text. Because RICO does not define “interest,” the Court assumed its ordinary meaning, which covers all forms of real and personal property, including profits and proceeds, and every property interest can be called an interest in something. Congress chose a broad term consistent with RICO's pattern of using general terms like enterprise and racketeering activity. The structure of the statute confirms this: subsection (a)(2) expressly limits forfeiture to an interest in an enterprise, and the omission of that limit from (a)(1) is presumed intentional, especially since an early bill's limiting phrase was dropped from the first subsection. The two subsections are not redundant, since (a)(1) reaches illegally acquired interests whether or not in an enterprise while (a)(2) reaches enterprise interests even if lawfully acquired. A narrow reading would blunt the provision against illegitimate enterprises, which rarely hold assets because proceeds are quickly distributed. The specific mention of profits in the Controlled Substances Act, subsequent bills, and state statutes did not limit the broad term. The legislative history shows Congress meant to attack organized crime's economic base, which depends on its profits, and directed that the statute be liberally construed. The rule of lenity does not apply because the language is clear, and “the rule of lenity does not come into play.”","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep464/usrep464016/usrep464016.pdf"},"1979/79-521":{"facts":"The Consumer Product Safety Commission obtained accident reports from manufacturers under the Consumer Product Safety Act (CPSA), most of them accompanied by claims of confidentiality. After the Consumers Union and Public Citizen's Health Research Group filed Freedom of Information Act (FOIA) requests, the Commission decided to release the reports, including those claimed to be confidential, without following section 6(b)(1) of the CPSA. That section requires 30 days' notice to a manufacturer whose identity the public could readily ascertain, a chance to comment, and reasonable steps to ensure the information is accurate and its disclosure fair.","procedure":"Manufacturers sued to block release. The District Court granted summary judgment and permanently enjoined disclosure, holding that section 6(b)(1) applies to FOIA releases and falls within FOIA Exemption 3. The Third Circuit affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict with the Second Circuit.","question":"Does section 6(b)(1) of the Consumer Product Safety Act govern the Commission's disclosure of records in response to a Freedom of Information Act request?","holding":"Yes. Section 6(b)(1) applies to public disclosures made in response to FOIA requests as well as to disclosures the Commission initiates. The Commission's practical objections did not change that result.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text, which covers the public disclosure of any information obtained under the Act, and FOIA requesters are part of the public. The exceptions in section 6(b)(2) do not include FOIA releases, although Congress knew of the FOIA and incorporated its exemptions in section 6(a)(1), and section 25(c) makes certain reports subject to section 6(b) whether released affirmatively or on request. The pre-enactment history showed industry and congressional concern about disclosure generally, and committee and administration comments drew no distinction between the two kinds of release. Later statements, including a remark by Representative Moss and a 1976 conference report, deserve little weight because they came from a later Congress or were isolated and unpersuasive, and the Commission's interpretation was recent and arguably litigation-driven. Section 6(b)(1) sets definite criteria, so it falls under FOIA Exemption 3, which defeats the claimed conflict with FOIA's time limits, and claims of administrative burden are for Congress.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Rehnquist delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447102/usrep447102.pdf"},"1981/80-846":{"facts":"Noah Lundy was convicted in a Tennessee court of rape and crime against nature and sentenced to long consecutive terms. After the state appellate courts affirmed and he lost a state postconviction petition, he sought federal habeas relief under 28 U.S.C. § 2254 on four grounds: a limit on cross-examination of the victim, a prosecutor's reference to his violent character, a prosecutor's closing remark that the State's evidence was uncontradicted, and a jury instruction that every witness is presumed to swear the truth. Only the first two had been presented to the state courts.","procedure":"The District Court said it could not consider the third and fourth claims because they were unexhausted, but it looked at them \"collaterally\" in assessing the trial's atmosphere, also reviewed other instances of prosecutorial misconduct never raised in state court, and granted the writ. The Sixth Circuit affirmed and rejected the State's argument that the petition should have been dismissed because it mixed exhausted and unexhausted claims. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the exhaustion requirement of 28 U.S.C. §§ 2254(b) and (c) require a federal district court to dismiss a habeas petition that contains both exhausted and unexhausted claims?","holding":"Yes. A district court must dismiss a \"mixed petition\" containing both exhausted and unexhausted claims, leaving the prisoner to return to state court to exhaust the rest or to amend or resubmit the petition with only exhausted claims.","reasoning":"The statute and its history do not address mixed petitions, so the Court looked to the purposes of the exhaustion doctrine, which rests on comity and on protecting the state courts' role in enforcing federal law. A total exhaustion rule gives state courts the first chance to review all federal claims, makes them more familiar with federal issues, and produces a fuller factual record for federal review. The District Court's own statement that the violations were mixed so that one could not be separated from the others showed how hard it is for courts to decide when claims are interrelated and how tempting it is to consider unexhausted ones. The Court viewed the rule as simple and clear for pro se prisoners and expected it to reduce piecemeal litigation. In a plurality part (O'Connor, joined by Burger, Powell and Rehnquist), the opinion added that the rule does not unduly delay relief because the prisoner may delete the unexhausted claims, though doing so risks dismissal of later petitions as an abuse of the writ under Habeas Rule 9(b).","separate":"Blackmun concurred in the judgment: he would have held that only the unexhausted claims of a mixed petition are dismissed while the district court rules on exhausted claims, argued that the Court's rule traps unwary pro se prisoners and adds delay, and would remand to dismiss the unexhausted claims and reconsider the rest. Brennan (joined by Marshall) joined the Court's opinion but not Part III-C, arguing that the Rule 9(b) abuse-of-the-writ issue was not before the Court and that a prisoner who drops unexhausted claims because the court refused a mixed petition cannot be said to have abused the writ. White concurred in part and dissented in part, agreeing with most of Brennan's opinion but, like Blackmun, preferring that the court rule on exhausted claims unless they are intertwined with the dismissed ones. Stevens dissented, arguing that the District Court properly judged the exhausted claims in the context of the whole trial, that district judges should have discretion over mixed petitions, and that the rule is needless procedural lawmaking that delays relief.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice O'Connor announced the judgment and wrote the opinion of the Court (Parts I, II, III-A, III-B and IV, joined by Burger, Brennan, Marshall, Powell and Rehnquist); Part III-C was a plurality opinion.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep455/usrep455509/usrep455509.pdf"},"1987/86-1461":{"facts":"A construction company allegedly paying substandard wages was building a department store for a tenant in a Tampa shopping mall owned by the DeBartolo Corporation. A union with a dispute against the contractor peacefully handed out leaflets at the mall's four entrances for about three weeks, asking customers not to shop at any mall store until the owner promised that mall construction would use contractors paying fair wages. The handbills said the union sought only a consumer boycott and did not ask anyone to stop work or deliveries, and there was no picketing or patrolling.","procedure":"On DeBartolo's charge under § 8(b)(4) of the National Labor Relations Act, the Board first dismissed the complaint under the statute's publicity proviso, but the Supreme Court held the proviso inapplicable and remanded. On remand the Board held the handbilling violated § 8(b)(4)(ii)(B) as coercion and declined to reach First Amendment issues. The Eleventh Circuit denied enforcement, construing the statute not to reach such handbilling to avoid serious constitutional doubts.","question":"Does § 8(b)(4)(ii)(B) of the NLRA, which bars unions from threatening, coercing, or restraining a neutral to cease doing business with another, prohibit peaceful handbilling urging a consumer boycott of a mall's stores, or should the statute be read to avoid the First Amendment question?","holding":"The statute can fairly be read not to reach peaceful handbilling, unaccompanied by picketing, urging a consumer boycott of neutral employers, and that reading avoids serious First Amendment questions. Neither the statutory language nor the legislative history clearly shows that Congress meant to prohibit such handbilling.","reasoning":"The Board's reading would ordinarily get deference, but the Court applied the rule that \"where an otherwise acceptable construction of a statute would raise serious constitutional problems, the Court will construe the statute to avoid such problems unless such construction is plainly contrary to the intent of Congress.\" The Board's reading raised serious First Amendment questions because the handbills truthfully disclosed a labor dispute and urged a lawful course of action. The words \"threaten, coerce, or restrain\" are vague and should be read with caution, and handbilling that relies on persuasion alone is different from picketing, which was the concern in Tree Fruits and Safeco. The publicity proviso can be read as a clarification of what is not coercive rather than an exception to a ban on all publicity, since the Board's reading would reach newspaper and radio appeals and even union meetings. The legislative history showed concern with secondary picketing, only opponents of the bill said it reached leaflets, and Senator Kennedy's remarks and the conference summary indicated that publicity short of picketing remained free.","separate":"Justices O'Connor and Scalia concurred in the judgment without a written opinion. Justice Kennedy took no part.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Rehnquist and Justices Brennan, Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep485/usrep485568/usrep485568.pdf"},"1980/79-1404":{"facts":"Pennsylvania owns and operates Pennhurst State School and Hospital, an institution housing about 1,200 mentally retarded residents. Terri Lee Halderman, a resident, sued on behalf of all residents in 1974, alleging that conditions at Pennhurst were unsanitary, inhumane and dangerous and seeking injunctive and monetary relief and the closing of Pennhurst in favor of community living arrangements. The United States and the Pennsylvania Association for Retarded Citizens intervened as plaintiffs. The District Court's findings about the conditions were undisputed, and Pennsylvania participated in the federal-state grant program created by the Developmentally Disabled Assistance and Bill of Rights Act, receiving about $1.6 million under it in 1976.","procedure":"After a 32-day trial the District Court certified a class and held that the residents had constitutional and statutory rights to minimally adequate habilitation in the least restrictive environment, ordering that Pennhurst eventually be closed. The en banc Third Circuit substantially affirmed, but avoided the constitutional claims and rested on 42 U.S.C. section 6010 of the Act, finding that it created substantive rights enforceable through an implied cause of action; it also relied on state law and did not require closing Pennhurst. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does section 6010, the “bill of rights” provision of the Developmentally Disabled Assistance and Bill of Rights Act, create in favor of the mentally retarded substantive rights to appropriate treatment in the least restrictive setting that bind participating States?","holding":"No. Section 6010 is a statement of congressional findings and policy that expresses a preference for certain kinds of treatment; it creates no substantive rights and does not condition federal funds on the States providing such treatment. The Court left open whether private parties may enforce the Act's specific conditions (sections 6011 and 6063(b)(5)(C)), and remanded the state-law, constitutional and Rehabilitation Act claims.","reasoning":"Congress's intent to legislate under section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment is not lightly inferred, especially where the asserted rights impose affirmative funding obligations; here the Act's language and structure show a funding statute under the spending power. Spending Clause legislation is much in the nature of a contract, so a condition on federal funds must be stated unambiguously so that States can accept knowingly. Section 6010 contains no conditional language, in contrast to sections 6005, 6009, 6011, 6012 and 6063, and the Court called it “too thin a reed to support the rights and obligations read into it by the court below.” The legislative history shows that the Conference Committee dropped the Senate's detailed standards and funding sanction, and the Secretary of HHS had not read section 6010 as a condition; the modest $1.6 million grant also pointed to a limited purpose. Comparison with the specific conditions in sections 6011, 6063(b)(5)(C) and 6062 showed that Congress knew how to impose duties and would have had no reason for them if section 6010 already imposed absolute obligations. The Court noted open questions about private enforcement, whether Pennhurst is a “program assisted”, remedy and the Eleventh Amendment, and remanded them.","separate":"Justice Blackmun concurred in part and in the judgment. He joined most of the opinion but not Part IV, objecting to its apparent negative attitude toward private enforcement of sections 6011 and 6063, and he thought section 6010 should be given some meaning through its link to section 6063 and that Pennhurst should not escape the Act because it receives no funds directly. Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented in part. He agreed the Act was passed under the spending power but argued that section 6010 and its legislative history establish requirements that participating States must meet, linked to the state-plan assurances in section 6063; he would hold the rights enforceable under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 per Maine v. Thiboutot, and would vacate and remand so that the lower court could follow the Rosado v. Wyman approach, giving the State a chance to comply or forgo federal funds, rather than supervising Pennhurst through a Special Master.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep451/usrep451001/usrep451001.pdf"},"1983/83-5596":{"facts":"Joseph Spaziano was indicted for first-degree murder two years and one month after the alleged offense in 1973; Florida had a two-year limitations period for noncapital offenses and none for capital ones. At the close of the evidence the trial court offered to instruct the jury on the lesser included noncapital offenses if Spaziano would waive the statute of limitations on them, and he refused after the court explained the consequences. The jury, instructed only on capital murder, convicted him of first-degree murder, and a majority at the advisory sentencing hearing recommended life imprisonment. The trial judge nonetheless imposed death, finding two aggravating circumstances (a heinous, atrocious and cruel killing and a prior violent felony) and no mitigating circumstances.","procedure":"The Florida Supreme Court affirmed the conviction, rejecting a Beck v. Alabama claim, but reversed the death sentence because the judge had relied on an undisclosed confidential part of the presentence report. On remand the trial court held a new hearing and again imposed death, and the Florida Supreme Court affirmed, finding no constitutional problem with the judge's override of the jury's life recommendation. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the trial court violate the Constitution by refusing to give a lesser included offense instruction unless the defendant waived the statute of limitations, and by overriding the jury's recommendation of life imprisonment to impose a death sentence?","holding":"No. Under Beck v. Alabama a defendant need not be forced to give up the limitations defense, and may instead choose between the instruction and the defense; Spaziano knowingly chose the defense. The Constitution does not require that a jury's life recommendation in a capital case be final or that the jury impose the sentence, so the Florida override procedure does not violate the Sixth, Eighth or Fifth Amendment, and its application here was not arbitrary.","reasoning":"Beck's concern is the distortion of the jury's fact-finding when it faces an all-or-nothing choice, and an instruction on offenses for which the defendant cannot be convicted would not improve rationality and would mislead the jury, so the defendant may choose between the instruction and the limitations defense. The Sixth Amendment has never been thought to guarantee a jury determination of the appropriate punishment, and a capital sentencing, though like a trial for double jeopardy purposes, involves the same fundamental sentencing issue. The Furman line of cases seeks measured, consistent application and individualized consideration, and nothing in those objectives requires a jury as sentencer; retribution and community expression are served by the legislature's definition of death-eligible crimes. That 30 of 37 capital jurisdictions give the decision to the jury does not make judicial sentencing offensive to contemporary standards of decency. Because a judge may be sole sentencer, jury advice is not a judgment for double jeopardy purposes: “The advice does not become a judgment simply because it comes from the jury.” As applied, the Tedder standard, the judge's written findings and mandatory Florida Supreme Court review gave no sign of arbitrariness, and the two aggravating circumstances supported the sentence.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Justice Rehnquist, concurred in part and in the judgment, joining everything except the dictum in Part II suggesting that Beck requires a state court to allow a defendant to waive limitations and receive a lesser included offense instruction on a time-barred offense. Justice Stevens, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, joined Part II but dissented from the rest. He argued that because the death penalty rests on retribution and the community's moral outrage, the Eighth Amendment, in light of history, near-universal state practice, Florida's origin of the override in an attempted response to Furman, and the greater ability of a representative jury to reflect community values, requires that the state persuade a jury that death is appropriate, so that overriding a life recommendation imposes a disproportionate punishment.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined in full by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Powell and O'Connor; Justices White and Rehnquist joined all but a portion of Part II, and Justices Brennan, Marshall and Stevens joined Part II only.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep468/usrep468447/usrep468447.pdf"},"1984/84-351":{"facts":"Douglas Scanlon, who has diabetes and no sight in one eye, alleged that in 1978 Atascadero State Hospital, a California state facility, refused to hire him as a graduate student assistant recreational therapist solely because of his physical handicaps. He sued the hospital and the California Department of Mental Health in federal court under § 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, seeking compensatory, injunctive and declaratory relief. Section 505 of the Act makes the remedies of Title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 available against “any recipient of Federal assistance.”","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the complaint as barred by the Eleventh Amendment. The Ninth Circuit first affirmed on a different ground, the Supreme Court vacated and remanded in light of Consolidated Rail Corp. v. Darrone, and on remand the Ninth Circuit reversed, holding that California had implicitly consented to suit by participating in and receiving funds under the Act. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict with the First and Eighth Circuits.","question":"Are States and state agencies subject to suit in federal court for retroactive monetary relief under § 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, or does the Eleventh Amendment bar such suits?","holding":"The Eleventh Amendment bars the suit. California did not waive its immunity through its constitution, the Rehabilitation Act does not clearly abrogate state immunity, and accepting federal funds under the Act does not show consent to suit in federal court.","reasoning":"Waiver and abrogation both require an unequivocal indication, because the Eleventh Amendment protects the constitutional balance between the States and the Federal Government and a State's interest covers not only whether but where it may be sued. Article III, § 5 of the California Constitution, allowing suits against the State “in such manner and in such courts as shall be directed by law,” does not specify federal court and merely authorizes the legislature to waive immunity. For abrogation, the Court held that Congress must express its intention “in unmistakable language in the statute itself,” a rule that ensures certainty and reflects that courts are deciding whether their own jurisdiction has been expanded. Section 504 and § 505 speak of remedies against “any recipient of Federal assistance,” but States are not like other recipients, and a general authorization for suit in federal court is not unequivocal enough. As to the Spending Clause theory, the Act falls far short of showing a clear intent to condition participation in its programs on a State's consent to waive immunity, so receipt of funds is not consent. The Court also defended Hans v. Louisiana and its line of cases against the dissents' calls to reexamine them.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens, dissented. He argued that the language and legislative history of § 504 show Congress plainly meant to bind and subject the States to suit, that the Court's clear-statement rules are hurdles designed to keep suits out of federal court rather than tools for finding congressional intent, and that the doctrine rests on mistaken history, offering a lengthy account of the ratification debates, Chisholm v. Georgia, the Eleventh Amendment's text and the Marshall Court cases to argue that neither Article III nor the Amendment embodies a general state sovereign immunity in federal-question cases. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall and Stevens, dissented separately, joining Justice Brennan, likening the Eleventh Amendment cases to the Tenth Amendment doctrine abandoned in Garcia, and stating that he would also find that California consented by accepting funds and that Congress acted under § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment. Justice Stevens dissented separately, explaining that he joined Justice Brennan's dissent and now regarded Edelman v. Jordan and Hans as incorrectly decided, because the Court had not felt bound by stare decisis in expanding sovereign immunity.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Rehnquist and O'Connor (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep473/usrep473234/usrep473234.pdf"},"1979/79-134":{"facts":"Consolidated Edison put a bill insert in its January 1976 billing envelopes arguing that the benefits of nuclear power far outweigh any risk. The Natural Resources Defense Council asked to include a rebuttal in a later envelope, and when Con Edison refused, it asked the New York Public Service Commission to open the envelopes to contrasting views. In 1977 the Commission denied that request but prohibited utilities from using bill inserts to discuss political matters, including nuclear power. It reasoned that customers receiving bills are a captive audience who should not be subjected to the utility's views on controversial public policy issues. It still allowed inserts on other topics, such as energy conservation.","procedure":"The state trial court held the order unconstitutional, but the Appellate Division reversed and the New York Court of Appeals affirmed, treating the order as a valid time, place, and manner regulation protecting customer privacy. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Does the Commission's order prohibiting utilities from including inserts discussing controversial issues of public policy in monthly bills violate the First and Fourteenth Amendments?","holding":"Yes. The order directly infringes protected speech and is neither a valid time, place, or manner restriction, nor a permissible subject-matter regulation, nor a precisely drawn means of serving a compelling state interest.","reasoning":"The Court first held that Con Edison's status as a corporation does not deprive it of free speech protection, following Bellotti, and that the ban \"strikes at the heart of the freedom to speak\" on public issues. A time, place, or manner rule must be content-neutral, but the Commission admitted it barred inserts because of their subject matter, allowing some topics and forbidding controversial issues. Applying the ban to all views on a subject does not save it, since the First Amendment's hostility to content regulation reaches prohibitions of an entire topic. Greer v. Spock and Lehman v. Shaker Heights were narrow exceptions concerning access to government property and did not apply to a private party using its own billing envelope. Under strict scrutiny, the captive-audience rationale failed because customers can avoid the message \"simply by transferring the bill insert from envelope to wastebasket.\" The limited-space rationale failed because envelopes are not a scarce resource like broadcast frequencies and the record did not show inserts would crowd out others. The cost-subsidy rationale failed because the Commission had not shown it could not allocate costs to shareholders and did not rest its order on that ground.","separate":"Justice Marshall concurred, stressing that the decision does not address whether the Commission may exclude insert costs from the rate base. Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, saying that the Court's statement that time, place, or manner rules may never rest on subject matter is overbroad; he would rest the decision on the point that a regulation motivated by a wish to curtail a particular point of view on controversial issues abridges speech, and that offensiveness of a message cannot justify censorship. Justice Blackmun dissented, joined by Justice Rehnquist as to Parts I and II. He argued that using the billing envelope for the utility's message is a forced subsidy by ratepayers of a state-created monopoly, that the State may prevent this, and that even cost allocation would not eliminate the free ride; he also noted ways States might still address the problem.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, and Marshall; Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, and Justice Blackmun dissented, with Justice Rehnquist joining Parts I and II.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447530/usrep447530.pdf"},"1985/84-6859":{"facts":"Petitioner Skipper was convicted of capital murder and rape in a South Carolina court, and a separate sentencing hearing was held before the jury. The State presented evidence of his history of sexually assaultive behavior. Skipper offered mitigating testimony from himself and from his former wife, mother, sister and grandmother, and he and his former wife briefly said he behaved well during the 7 1/2 months he spent in jail awaiting trial. He also sought to call two jailers and a regular visitor to testify that he had made a good adjustment in jail, but the trial court excluded this as irrelevant under State v. Koon. In closing, the prosecutor argued that Skipper would pose disciplinary problems and would likely rape other prisoners. The jury sentenced him to death.","procedure":"The South Carolina Supreme Court affirmed the death sentence, stating that evidence of future adaptability to prison was properly refused and that evidence of past adaptability had come in through other witnesses. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari to consider whether this conflicted with Lockett v. Ohio and Eddings v. Oklahoma.","question":"Did the trial court's exclusion of testimony from jailers and a visitor about the defendant's good conduct in jail deny him the right to place relevant mitigating evidence before the capital sentencing jury?","holding":"Yes. Under Lockett and Eddings, the sentencer may not be precluded from considering any relevant mitigating evidence, and evidence that a defendant would behave well in prison if spared is potentially mitigating. Excluding the testimony was error that could not be treated as harmless, so the death sentence could not stand.","reasoning":"The Court began with the well-established rule that the sentencer may not refuse to consider \"any relevant mitigating evidence.\" Testimony that Skipper had been a well-behaved prisoner could support favorable inferences about his character and probable future conduct in prison, and so could serve as a basis for a sentence less than death; predicting future conduct is an inevitable part of sentencing, and future dangerousness may be treated as an aggravating factor. The Court rejected the State's three arguments. The record did not show the ruling was merely an application of rules against lay opinion, since counsel expected the witnesses to testify that he had made a good adjustment. The State's distinction between past conduct offered for character and future adaptability was elusive, and the ruling in fact barred the evidence for any purpose. Nor was the evidence merely cumulative, since the jury would tend to discount the self-serving testimony of Skipper and his family but give greater weight to disinterested jailers, particularly as the prosecutor stressed Skipper's dangerousness in prison. The Court did not hold that every facet of prison adjustment is relevant. It added that due process also requires an opportunity to respond to a prosecutor's reliance on future dangerousness.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, concurred in the judgment. He would reverse on the narrower ground of Gardner v. Florida, because Skipper was not allowed to rebut the prosecutor's evidence and argument about his conduct in jail. He disagreed that Lockett and Eddings reach post-arrest conduct, which does not reduce culpability for the crime, argued that States should decide what evidence is relevant, and said that States could reasonably exclude good jail behavior as having little predictive value and as undermining deterrence.","disposition":"The judgment of the South Carolina Supreme Court was reversed insofar as it affirmed the death sentence, and the case was remanded for further proceedings, with the State free to seek death again through a new sentencing hearing. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, Blackmun, Stevens and O'Connor. Justice Powell concurred in the judgment, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep476/usrep476001/usrep476001.pdf"},"1979/78-6621":{"facts":"Beck was tried in Alabama for the capital offense of robbery in which the victim is intentionally killed by the defendant. His own testimony established that he took part in the robbery of an 80-year-old man, Roy Malone, but he consistently denied that he killed Malone or intended his death; he said his accomplice unexpectedly struck and killed the man. Under Alabama's death penalty statute, the judge was prohibited from instructing the jury on lesser included offenses, even though felony murder was a lesser included offense and the State conceded the testimony would otherwise have entitled him to that instruction.","procedure":"The jury could only convict of the capital crime, which required a death sentence, or acquit. It convicted him and fixed the death penalty, and after a hearing on aggravating and mitigating circumstances the trial judge refused to overturn the sentence. The Alabama Court of Criminal Appeals and the Alabama Supreme Court rejected his constitutional attack on the prohibition, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a death sentence constitutionally be imposed after a jury verdict of guilt of a capital offense when the jury was not permitted to consider a verdict of guilt of a lesser included noncapital offense, and the evidence would have supported such a verdict?","holding":"No. A death sentence may not constitutionally be imposed after a jury verdict of guilt of a capital offense where the jury was not permitted to consider a verdict of guilt of a lesser included noncapital offense that the evidence would support.","reasoning":"The Court explained that a lesser included offense instruction gives the jury a \"third option\" that ensures the defendant receives the full benefit of the reasonable-doubt standard; without it, a jury convinced the defendant is guilty of some serious crime but doubtful about an element of the capital offense is likely to resolve doubts in favor of conviction. Alabama's practice was unique, since federal courts, other states, and Alabama itself in noncapital cases require such instructions when evidence warrants. Because death is different, and the Court had demanded reliability in capital sentencing, the same concern applies to the guilt determination. It rejected Alabama's argument that an apparently mandatory death penalty makes juries more careful, finding that the statute \"interjects irrelevant considerations into the factfinding process\", encouraging conviction because the jury thinks the defendant deserves punishment, or acquittal because he does not deserve death, with no guiding standards. A mistrial is no adequate substitute, and the judge's later sentencing hearing cannot be presumed to correct a flawed verdict, since the judge would never reach the question if the jury had convicted only of a lesser offense and the jury's verdict tends to influence the judge.","separate":"Justice Brennan concurred, adhering to his view that the death penalty is always cruel and unusual punishment. Justice Marshall concurred in the judgment, likewise adhering to that view while agreeing the prohibition on lesser included offense instructions is unconstitutional because it increases the risk of error. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice White, dissented, arguing the Court lacked jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. section 1257 because the Alabama Supreme Court's opinion addressed only state constitutional issues and it is assumed a federal question not passed on was not properly presented below.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, Blackmun, and Powell; Justice Marshall concurred in the judgment; Justices Rehnquist and White dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447625/usrep447625.pdf"},"1984/84-335":{"facts":"In 1970 the FBI learned that members of an antiwar group planned to blow up heating tunnels linking federal buildings in Washington and had discussed kidnapping the National Security Adviser. Attorney General John Mitchell authorized a warrantless wiretap on the phone of Professor William Davidon, a group member, for national security intelligence. From late November 1970 to January 1971 the tap intercepted three apparently innocuous conversations between Davidon and Keith Forsyth, who learned of it in 1972. After this Court held in Keith that the Fourth Amendment forbids warrantless wiretaps in domestic national security cases, Forsyth sued Mitchell for damages under the Fourth Amendment and Title III.","procedure":"The District Court rejected Mitchell's claims of absolute immunity and, applying Harlow v. Fitzgerald, held he was not entitled to qualified immunity because Keith merely extended settled principles; it granted Forsyth summary judgment on liability. The Third Circuit affirmed the denial of absolute immunity but held the denial of qualified immunity was not immediately appealable under the collateral order doctrine. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is the Attorney General absolutely immune from damages suits for authorizing warrantless wiretaps for national security purposes? Is a district court's denial of qualified immunity immediately appealable before final judgment? And was Mitchell entitled to qualified immunity?","holding":"The Attorney General is not absolutely immune for his national security functions. A denial of qualified immunity, to the extent it turns on a question of law, is an appealable final decision under 28 U.S.C. section 1291. Mitchell was entitled to qualified immunity because the wiretap's illegality was not clearly established in 1970.","reasoning":"On absolute immunity, the Court said Cabinet status alone does not suffice and Mitchell identified no historical or common-law basis for immunity for national security tasks. Such tasks are carried out in secret and do not generate the same vexatious litigation as judicial functions, and they lack built-in checks that correct abuses, so the risk that officials disregard rights in their zeal to protect security counsels against absolute immunity. On appealability, qualified immunity under Harlow is \"an immunity from suit rather than a mere defense to liability\" and like absolute immunity is effectively lost if the case goes to trial; denial conclusively decides the question and the appealable issue, whether the law was clearly established, is a legal one conceptually separate from the merits. On the merits, the Court reviewed the long executive practice of warrantless national security wiretaps, Katz's express reservation of the national security question, Title III's neutrality, and two district court decisions upholding such wiretaps. It concluded the question was open in 1970, and \"The decisive fact is not that Mitchell's position turned out to be incorrect\"; the District Court's reasoning that he gambled and lost was the hindsight Harlow rejected.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in part, joining Parts I, III, and IV, but would have held that the Attorney General has absolute immunity for national security acts as an aide to the President. Justice O'Connor, joined by the Chief Justice, concurred in part, joining Parts I, III, and IV and the judgment but declining to reach absolute immunity. Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, arguing that Mitchell was acting under the President's delegated power and, since Congress declined to create a damages remedy for such wiretaps, he is entitled to absolute immunity. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, joined Parts I and II but dissented on appealability, contending a qualified immunity denial is neither completely separate from the merits nor effectively unreviewable after final judgment, and that the Court mischaracterized the record because the District Court never resolved whether the wiretap was truly for national security.","disposition":"Affirmed in part and reversed in part: the denial of absolute immunity was affirmed, and the Court of Appeals' judgment was reversed to the extent it left in place the denial of qualified immunity. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Justice Blackmun joined; Chief Justice Burger and Justice O'Connor joined Parts I, III, and IV; Justices Brennan and Marshall joined Parts I and II. Justices Powell and Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep472/usrep472511/usrep472511.pdf"},"1986/85-782":{"facts":"Respondent, a Nicaraguan citizen, entered the United States in 1979 as a visitor and overstayed, and the INS began deportation proceedings. She conceded she was in the country illegally but sought withholding of deportation under section 243(h) and asylum as a refugee under section 208(a). Her evidence centered on her brother, who had been tortured and imprisoned in Nicaragua for political activity; she and he testified that the Sandinistas would interrogate her about him and learn of her own opposition to them, and she claimed she would be tortured if returned.","procedure":"The Immigration Judge applied the same standard to both claims, found no \"clear probability of persecution\" and denied both forms of relief; the Board of Immigration Appeals affirmed. The Ninth Circuit, where she pressed only the asylum claim, held that the \"well-founded fear\" standard is more generous than the clear-probability standard and remanded to the BIA, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the circuits.","question":"Must an alien show a clear probability (more likely than not) of persecution, the standard for mandatory withholding of deportation under section 243(h), to establish the \"well-founded fear of persecution\" that makes her a refugee eligible for discretionary asylum under section 208(a)?","holding":"No. The section 243(h) clear-probability standard does not govern asylum eligibility under section 208(a). An applicant need not prove that persecution is more likely than not in order to show a well-founded fear of persecution. The Court did not attempt to define in detail how the well-founded fear test is to be applied.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text: section 243(h) (\"would be threatened\") has no subjective component and requires objective proof that persecution is more likely than not, whereas \"fear\" in the refugee definition turns partly on the applicant's state of mind. It reasoned that \"One can certainly have a well-founded fear of an event happening when there is less than a 50% chance of the occurrence taking place.\" and that Congress, which drafted section 208(a) and amended section 243(h) at the same time, kept different language in the two provisions. Legislative history confirmed this: the pre-1980 conditional-entry statute used a more lenient fear-based standard, Congress meant to conform the Act to the United Nations Protocol, whose \"well-founded fear\" definition was understood to require only good reason or a reasonable possibility of persecution, and Congress adopted the House bill rather than the Senate version that would have limited asylum to those entitled to section 243(h) relief. The Court rejected the INS argument that it is anomalous for the asylum standard to be easier, because asylum remains discretionary while withholding is mandatory. Finally, it declined to defer to the BIA, because whether the standards are identical is \"a pure question of statutory construction for the courts to decide,\" distinct from the case-by-case application of the term to facts, and the BIA's own position had been inconsistent.","separate":"Justice Blackmun concurred, stressing that the Court's opinion points the INS to the sources (the statutory language and its subjective and objective components, the Protocol and its history) from which to develop the meaning of well-founded fear, and criticizing the INS's earlier reading. Justice Scalia concurred in the judgment only: he thought the plain meaning and structure of the Act settled the matter, so the long legislative-history inquiry and the discussion of deference to the agency were unnecessary, and he believed the Court's remarks on deference under Chevron misread that decision. Justice Powell, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice White, dissented: he argued that the BIA's Matter of Acosta interpretation (a four-part test requiring a realistic likelihood of persecution) was reasonable and deserved deference, that the statutory language and legislative history are ambiguous, and that in any event the BIA in this case had applied even the more lenient standard, so the Ninth Circuit should be reversed.","disposition":"Affirmed (Ninth Circuit judgment upholding the remand to the BIA). Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, Blackmun, and O'Connor; Justice Scalia concurred in the judgment; Justices Powell, White, and the Chief Justice dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep480/usrep480421/usrep480421.pdf"},"1980/83_orig":{"facts":"In 1978 Louisiana enacted a \"First-Use Tax\" of seven cents per thousand cubic feet on natural gas brought into the state that had not already been taxed by another State or the United States. Its main effect fell on gas from the federal Outer Continental Shelf (OCS) piped to Louisiana processing plants and mostly sold to consumers in over 30 other States. The tax was imposed on pipeline companies but was designed to be passed on to consumers, and the Act declared it a cost of preparing the gas for market and barred shifting it to anyone except the ultimate consumer. Exemptions and credits largely shielded Louisiana users of OCS gas.","procedure":"Several States, later joined by the United States, FERC and pipeline companies, sued in the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction, claiming violations of the Supremacy and Commerce Clauses. The Special Master recommended denying Louisiana's motion to dismiss but also denying the plaintiffs' motion for judgment on the pleadings pending evidentiary hearings. Exceptions to the reports were argued before the Court.","question":"Is this an appropriate case for the Court's original jurisdiction despite pending state-court suits, and, if so, does the First-Use Tax violate the Supremacy Clause or the Commerce Clause so that judgment may be entered without further hearings?","holding":"The Court accepted original jurisdiction and held that § 1303C of the Act violates the Supremacy Clause and that the First-Use Tax is unconstitutional under the Commerce Clause because it discriminates against interstate commerce. Judgment enjoining collection was ordered without further hearings.","reasoning":"The plaintiff States have standing as major gas purchasers whose costs rose as a direct result of the tax, which was clearly meant to be passed on, and also as parens patriae for citizens who cannot sue for refunds in Louisiana. The Court found the case distinguishable from Arizona v. New Mexico: the plaintiff States were not parties to the state suits, the state forum could not give interim injunctive relief, and the tax reached millions of consumers in over 30 States and implicated federal OCS interests. On the Supremacy Clause, determining pipeline and producer costs belongs to FERC in the first instance, and § 1303C, by dictating that the tax is a processing cost passed to consumers and barring reimbursement from owners of extracted hydrocarbons, usurps that authority, as in Northern Natural Gas. On the Commerce Clause, the gas flow is continuous interstate commerce, and although \"interstate commerce may constitutionally be made to pay its way\", the exemptions and credits protect Louisiana consumers and producers while the tax burdens gas leaving the state. The tax cannot be a compensatory tax because Louisiana has no sovereign interest in compensation for severance from federally owned OCS land, and exact degrees of discrimination need not be shown.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, saying there is much validity in Justice Rehnquist's dissent and the Court should stay alert to efforts to expand original jurisdiction, but he joined the opinion. Justice Rehnquist dissented, arguing the case was not appropriate for original jurisdiction: a State suing only as a consumer or on behalf of consumers asserts no sovereign interest, parens patriae standing requires sovereign or quasi-sovereign interests, and the same issues were being litigated in Louisiana state courts, so he would have dismissed. He did not reach the merits.","disposition":"Exceptions to the Special Master's reports sustained in part and overruled in part; judgment for the plaintiffs holding the tax invalid and enjoining further collection, with jurisdiction retained to implement it. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens. Justice Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep451/usrep451725/usrep451725.pdf"},"1979/79-838":{"facts":"Lionel and Joline Thiboutot, married with eight children, three of whom are Lionel's from a prior marriage, received AFDC welfare benefits in Maine. The Maine Department of Human Services told Lionel that, in computing his benefits for the three children who are solely his, it would no longer allow for money spent supporting the other five children, even though he is legally obligated to support them. The Thiboutots contended this violated the federal Social Security Act, specifically 42 U.S.C. section 602(a)(7).","procedure":"After exhausting state administrative remedies, the Thiboutots sought review in the Maine Superior Court and added a claim under 42 U.S.C. section 1983. The court enjoined the rule, ordered new regulations and retroactive and prospective payments, but denied attorney's fees. The Maine Supreme Judicial Court held that there was no fee entitlement under state law but that fees were available under 42 U.S.C. section 1988, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does 42 U.S.C. section 1983, which covers deprivations of rights \"secured by the Constitution and laws,\" provide a remedy for purely statutory violations of federal law such as the Social Security Act, and if so may attorney's fees be awarded under section 1988 in such an action, including one brought in state court?","holding":"Yes to both. Section 1983 encompasses claims based on purely statutory violations of federal law, and section 1988 authorizes attorney's fees to the prevailing party in such actions, whether brought in federal or state court.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan reasoned that because Congress attached no modifiers to \"and laws,\" \"the plain language of the statute undoubtedly embraces respondents' claim that petitioners violated the Social Security Act.\" He added that any ambiguity was resolved by earlier decisions, including Edelman, Monell, Owen and Greenwood, which suggested that the section 1983 remedy covers statutory as well as constitutional violations, and by several Social Security Act cases where section 1983 was necessarily the only statutory cause of action. The legislative history of the 1874 addition of \"and laws\" is not definitive, and the Court found that Congress knew what it was doing, so arguments that it did not intend the result are for Congress. Because section 1988 makes fees available in any section 1983 action and makes no exception for statutory claims, section 1988 \"plainly applies to this suit,\" and the Fees Act's legislative history, including statements of sponsors and committee reports, is consistent. Because state courts may hear section 1983 actions and Congress treated fees as an integral part of the remedy, the Supremacy Clause makes fees available in state court, and no Eleventh Amendment issue arises there.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, dissented. He argued that \"and laws\" is not plain and that the historical record shows it was added inadvertently in the 1874 revision and meant only federal equal rights legislation, that the Court's reading separates section 1983 from its jurisdictional counterpart in 28 U.S.C. section 1343(3), that prior decisions only assumed the point in dicta, and that the ruling will expose state and local officials to broad liability and attorney's fee awards across many federal-state programs. He would have reversed.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens; Justice Powell dissented, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep448/usrep448001/usrep448001.pdf"},"1982/81-2101":{"facts":"Terri Lee Halderman, a resident of Pennhurst State School and Hospital, a Pennsylvania institution for the mentally retarded, sued in 1974 on behalf of a class of present and future residents, joined by the Pennsylvania Association for Retarded Citizens and the United States. The defendants were Pennhurst and its officials, the state Department of Public Welfare and its officials, and officials of five surrounding counties. The complaint alleged that conditions at Pennhurst violated the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments, section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, the federal Developmentally Disabled Assistance and Bill of Rights Act, and the Pennsylvania Mental Health and Mental Retardation Act of 1966 (MH/MR Act). The District Court found the conditions dangerous and inadequate for habilitation, and the findings were undisputed.","procedure":"The District Court granted relief on several grounds, including the MH/MR Act, ordered residents moved to community settings, and appointed a Special Master. The Court of Appeals first rested its affirmance on the federal Bill of Rights Act; this Court reversed that ruling in 1981 and remanded. On remand the Court of Appeals again affirmed, relying solely on the state statute as read by the Pennsylvania Supreme Court, and rejected the argument that the Eleventh Amendment barred the pendent state-law claim.","question":"Does the Eleventh Amendment bar a federal court from ordering state officials to conform their conduct to state law, including when the state-law claim is brought under the court's pendent jurisdiction?","holding":"Yes. A claim that state officials violated state law in carrying out their official duties is a claim against the State, and the Eleventh Amendment bars a federal court from granting injunctive or other relief on that basis. The bar applies equally to state-law claims heard under pendent jurisdiction.","reasoning":"The Court treated sovereign immunity as a constitutional limit on federal judicial power, and said a suit against officials is really against the State when the relief would operate against the State. Ex parte Young and Edelman v. Jordan allow prospective relief against officials only because they vindicate the supremacy of federal law; that rationale disappears when the claim rests on state law. The Court wrote that \"it is difficult to think of a greater intrusion on state sovereignty than when a federal court instructs state officials on how to conform their conduct to state law.\" It rejected the dissent's view that violating a state statute makes an official's act ultra vires, relying on Larson v. Domestic & Foreign Commerce Corp., which holds that error in exercising delegated power is not enough. Earlier pendent-jurisdiction cases that granted relief against state officials had not addressed the Eleventh Amendment and so did not control. The Court concluded that \"neither pendent jurisdiction nor any other basis of jurisdiction may override the Eleventh Amendment,\" and said that concerns about convenience or split litigation cannot override that limit. Relief against the county officials alone would be partial and incomplete and would not serve the purposes of pendent jurisdiction.","separate":"Justice Brennan dissented separately. He joined Justice Stevens's dissent but adhered to his view that the Eleventh Amendment bars only suits against States by citizens of other States, so Pennsylvania could not invoke it against its own citizens. He added that any nonconstitutional sovereign immunity would not cover conduct prohibited by state law. Justice Stevens, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, and Blackmun, dissented at length. He argued that the Court's holding contradicts a long line of cases (he said the Court \"repudiates at least 28 cases\"), that sovereign immunity has never shielded officials acting contrary to their sovereign's law, and that Larson recognizes conduct the sovereign has forbidden as ultra vires. He also argued that the ruling undercuts the Siler practice of deciding cases on state-law grounds to avoid federal constitutional questions, and that the Court's 1981 remand had directed the Court of Appeals to consider the state-law claim.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded, in an opinion by Justice Powell joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Rehnquist, and O'Connor. On remand the Court of Appeals may consider whether the judgment can be sustained on the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments, section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, or the Developmentally Disabled Assistance and Bill of Rights Act.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep465/usrep465089/usrep465089.pdf"},"1982/81-1203":{"facts":"Moses H. Cone Memorial Hospital, in North Carolina, contracted with Mercury Construction Corp., an Alabama contractor, to build additions to the hospital. The contract sent disputes first to an architect and then allowed binding arbitration under a broad arbitration clause. Mercury submitted claims for delay and impact costs to the architect, and after some discussion the Hospital's counsel said it would pay nothing and intended to file a declaratory judgment action in state court. The Hospital then sued Mercury and the architect in state court, seeking a declaration that there was no right to arbitration, and obtained an ex parte state-court injunction against Mercury's taking steps toward arbitration, which was dissolved when Mercury objected.","procedure":"Once the state injunction was lifted, Mercury filed a diversity action in federal district court to compel arbitration under section 4 of the Arbitration Act. The District Court stayed that action pending the state suit because the two involved the identical issue of arbitrability. The en banc Fourth Circuit held it had jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. section 1291, reversed the stay, and remanded with instructions to enter an order to arbitrate. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Was the District Court's stay of the federal arbitration suit pending parallel state litigation an appealable final decision under section 1291, and if so, did the stay properly rest on the Colorado River doctrine allowing a federal court to defer to concurrent state proceedings?","holding":"The stay was appealable, and it was an abuse of discretion. A stay is final when its sole purpose and effect are to surrender jurisdiction of a federal suit to a state court, because the plaintiff is then effectively out of court. Under the Colorado River exceptional-circumstances test, none of the relevant factors justified the stay, and the Court of Appeals acted within its authority in deciding arbitrability.","reasoning":"On appealability, the Court relied on Idlewild Liquor Corp. v. Epstein: because arbitrability was the only substantive issue in the federal suit and the state judgment would be res judicata, Mercury was \"effectively out of court\", so the stay amounted to a dismissal; alternatively, the order met the Cohen collateral-order criteria. On the merits, it reaffirmed that the decision whether to defer to parallel state litigation rests on \"a careful balancing of the important factors as they apply in a given case, with the balance heavily weighted in favor of the exercise of jurisdiction\", and found that the Colorado River test survived Will v. Calvert Fire Insurance Co. Neither court had jurisdiction over property and the federal forum was not inconvenient. The risk of piecemeal litigation did not favor a stay, because any split between arbitrating and litigating the Hospital's related dispute with the architect results from the Arbitration Act itself, not from the choice of court. Priority of filing was measured by progress in the two cases, and the federal case was ahead, while the stay frustrated the Act's policy of rapid enforcement. Federal law governs arbitrability and favors arbitration, the state court's ability to compel arbitration under section 4 was doubtful, and a stay is as much a refusal to exercise jurisdiction as a dismissal. Finally, because the parties had fully briefed arbitrability and no factual issues required a jury, the Court of Appeals acted within its authority in deciding it.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice O'Connor, dissented. He argued that the stay was an interlocutory order, tentative and subject to change, that it was not final under section 1291, and that Idlewild could not survive Coopers & Lybrand v. Livesay. He also argued that the Cohen exception did not apply and that the Court of Appeals should not have decided arbitrability, an issue the District Court never decided. He would have vacated and remanded with directions to dismiss the appeal, or alternatively reversed insofar as the Court of Appeals decided arbitrability.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices White, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell, and Stevens (6-3).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep460/usrep460001/usrep460001.pdf"},"1983/81-2159":{"facts":"Karen Silkwood worked as a laboratory analyst at a federally licensed plutonium fuel plant in Oklahoma operated by Kerr-McGee. Over a three-day period in November 1974 she was contaminated by plutonium from the plant, and her apartment was also contaminated, requiring many belongings to be destroyed. She was killed in an unrelated automobile accident a week later. Her father, as administrator of her estate, sued Kerr-McGee in a diversity action under Oklahoma tort law. Evidence showed some failures to comply with federal regulations as well as general compliance. The jury awarded $505,000 in actual damages and $10 million in punitive damages.","procedure":"The Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit held that Oklahoma's workers' compensation law was the exclusive remedy for the personal injuries and reversed that award. It affirmed the property damage award, but reversed the punitive damages on the ground that federal law pre-empted them. The estate appealed to the Supreme Court, which postponed the question of its jurisdiction until argument on the merits.","question":"Is a state-authorized award of punitive damages for injuries arising from the escape of plutonium from a federally licensed nuclear facility pre-empted by federal law, and was the case properly before the Court on appeal?","holding":"The case was not within the Court's appellate jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. section 1254(2), but the Court treated the filing as a petition for certiorari and granted it. The Court held that the punitive damages award is not pre-empted by federal law.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction, the Court of Appeals invalidated the award on pre-emption grounds without ruling on the Oklahoma statute authorizing punitive damages, so the statute was left untouched and an appeal under section 1254(2) was not available. On the merits, the Court acknowledged its recent holding in Pacific Gas & Electric that the federal government has occupied the field of nuclear safety regulation. It concluded, however, that \"the pre-empted field does not extend as far as Kerr-McGee would have it.\" The Court found ample evidence that Congress did not intend to bar state-law remedies for those injured by radiation, noting that Congress provided no federal remedy and that the legislative history of the Price-Anderson Act assumed that state tort remedies would remain available. It placed on Kerr-McGee the burden of showing that Congress meant to preclude punitive damages and found nothing in the history or regulations to do so. The Court accepted the tension between exclusive federal safety regulation and state damages awards as one Congress was willing to tolerate. For damages for radiation injuries, pre-emption turns on \"whether there is an irreconcilable conflict between the federal and state standards\" or whether the state standard would frustrate federal objectives. It found no such conflict: paying both federal fines and punitive damages is not physically impossible, and punitive damages do not frustrate the purpose of promoting nuclear power, which Congress did not intend to promote through means lacking adequate remedies for injured persons, or its intent to avoid dual regulation.","separate":"Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented and also joined Justice Powell's dissent. He argued that under Pacific Gas the purpose of a state rule determines pre-emption, that punitive damages are meant to regulate safety and so fall within the pre-empted field while compensatory damages do not, and that the Court wrongly treated the case as if it concerned whether injured victims could be compensated at all. Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Marshall and Blackmun, dissented. He argued that punitive damages are regulatory in nature, that Pacific Gas held the entire field of nuclear safety pre-empted except powers expressly ceded to the States, and that the Court wrongly shifted the burden to the party asserting pre-emption. He also argued that the award conflicts with the comprehensive federal scheme administered by the NRC, which found no significant violation, and that lay juries should not impose such penalties.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals as to punitive damages reversed and the case remanded, leaving Kerr-McGee free to press its other claims on remand (including insufficiency of the evidence and excessiveness of the amount). Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Rehnquist, Stevens, and O'Connor (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep464/usrep464238/usrep464238.pdf"},"1983/82-975":{"facts":"Section 28.04 of the Los Angeles Municipal Code prohibits posting signs on public property such as utility poles and lampposts. During a 1979 campaign for the Los Angeles City Council, Taxpayers for Vincent, a group of a candidate's supporters, hired Candidates' Outdoor Graphics Service (COGS) to make and post signs reading \"Roland Vincent-City Council.\" COGS attached cardboard signs to the crosswires of utility poles. City employees routinely removed all posters on covered objects, including the COGS signs, which were 48 of 1,207 signs removed in one week; most others appeared to be commercial. Taxpayers and COGS sued the city and officials, claiming the ordinance violated the First Amendment.","procedure":"The District Court made findings of fact, held the ordinance constitutional, and granted summary judgment to the City. The Court of Appeals reversed, reasoning that the ordinance was presumptively unconstitutional and that the City had not justified a total ban on signs based on its asserted interests in esthetics, traffic safety, and the use of public property. The City appealed to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does a city ordinance banning the posting of signs on public property violate the First Amendment, either on its face under the overbreadth doctrine or as applied to the posting of political campaign signs on utility poles?","holding":"No. The overbreadth doctrine did not apply on this record, and the ordinance is not unconstitutional as applied to appellees. A content-neutral, impartially administered ban on posting signs on public property is justified by the city's substantial esthetic interest in eliminating visual clutter and is not broader than necessary to serve that interest.","reasoning":"The Court first declined to entertain an overbreadth challenge because the record showed no realistic danger that the ordinance would compromise the First Amendment interests of third parties any differently than appellees' own, so it analyzed the ordinance as applied. Because the ordinance is viewpoint-neutral, with no hint of bias in its text or enforcement, the Court applied the O'Brien framework and found the city's interest in improving its appearance substantial, citing Metromedia. It described the problem as \"the visual assault on the citizens of Los Angeles presented by an accumulation of signs posted on public property\", which is a substantive evil within the city's power to prohibit. The Court reasoned that the ban was narrowly tailored: \"The ordinance curtails no more speech than is necessary to accomplish its purpose\", because the blight is created by the medium of expression itself, unlike the leafleting in Schneider v. State. The ban need not extend to private property, since the private owner's interest in controlling that property justifies different treatment. Ample alternative channels remained, and the public property at issue was not a public forum under Perry. Finally, the Constitution did not require an exception for political campaign signs, since such an exception might itself risk content discrimination and undermine the ordinance's aim.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Marshall and Blackmun, dissented. He argued that the Court's approach is too lenient toward speech restrictions justified by esthetics, because esthetic judgments are subjective and can mask suppression of content or be redefined to fit the means chosen. He maintained that signs on public property are an important and inexpensive medium, especially for poorly financed speakers, and that alternatives were not shown to be adequate. He would require a city to show that it pursues its esthetic goal seriously and comprehensively, and because Los Angeles showed no efforts beyond the sign ban, he would hold the total ban invalid.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Stevens wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Powell, Rehnquist, and O'Connor (6-3).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep466/usrep466789/usrep466789.pdf"},"1984/83-1623":{"facts":"In 1975 Bessemer City, North Carolina, sought to hire a Recreation Director, and a five-member committee of four men and one woman made the choice. Eight people applied, including petitioner, a 39-year-old schoolteacher and the only woman applicant. The committee offered the job to a 24-year-old man with a college degree in physical education. All four men voted for him, and only the woman member voted for petitioner. Petitioner filed discrimination charges with the EEOC, received a right-to-sue letter, and sued under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964.","procedure":"After a two-day trial, the District Court found that petitioner was denied the job because of her sex, based on findings that she was better qualified, that she alone was asked about her husband's feelings about her applying, and that the male committee members were biased and their reasons pretextual. It awarded backpay and attorney's fees. The Fourth Circuit reversed, holding three of those findings clearly erroneous, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the Court of Appeals properly apply the clearly-erroneous standard of Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 52(a) in setting aside the District Court's finding of sex discrimination under Title VII?","holding":"No. The Court of Appeals misapprehended and misapplied the clearly-erroneous standard. A reviewing court may not reverse a plausible finding merely because it would have weighed the evidence differently, and the District Court's findings, including the finding of discrimination, were not clearly erroneous.","reasoning":"The Court first rejected the argument that the District Court's findings deserved closer scrutiny because the court asked petitioner's counsel to propose findings. The court had supplied the framework, allowed the city to respond, and issued findings that differed considerably from those proposed, so they were its own conclusions. A finding of intentional discrimination is a finding of fact reviewed under Rule 52(a), and a finding is clearly erroneous only when the reviewing court is left with a definite and firm conviction that a mistake was made. The Court said that \"Where there are two permissible views of the evidence, the factfinder's choice between them cannot be clearly erroneous\", even when findings rest on documents or inferences rather than credibility, and that greater deference applies to credibility determinations. Applying these principles, the Fourth Circuit effectively weighed the evidence anew on petitioner's qualifications, and failed to give due regard to the District Court's assessment of Mrs. Boone's testimony about the questioning. Given that finding, the finding of bias was supported by the interview, one member's view that the job would be hard for a woman, and another member's soliciting applications only from men. Those findings sufficed to support the inference of sex discrimination.","separate":"Justice Powell concurred, writing that the opinion should not be read to criticize the Court of Appeals for undertaking a comprehensive review of the record, and that the question could fairly be decided for either party, so the District Court's decision was not clearly erroneous. Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment only, declining to join the Court's statement that the same deferential standard applies when findings rest wholly on documentary evidence, which he thought unnecessary to decide in this case.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Marshall, Powell, Rehnquist, Stevens, and O'Connor; Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep470/usrep470564/usrep470564.pdf"},"1982/81-1893":{"facts":"Marcelino Ramos took part in the robbery of a fast-food restaurant where he worked as a janitor. He took out a gun, ordered the two employees into a walk-in refrigerator, struck both and shot them, killing one. A jury convicted him of robbery, attempted murder and first-degree murder and found a robbery-murder special circumstance true, which made him eligible for death or life without parole. At the separate penalty phase, California law required the judge to give the Briggs Instruction, telling jurors that the Governor may commute a sentence of life imprisonment without possibility of parole to one that includes the possibility of parole. The jury returned a death verdict.","procedure":"The California Supreme Court affirmed the conviction but reversed the death sentence, holding that the Briggs Instruction violated the Federal Constitution because it injected a speculative factor foreign to the jury's task and did not also tell the jury that a death sentence may be commuted. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Eighth or Fourteenth Amendment forbid a State from instructing a capital sentencing jury that the Governor may commute a sentence of life without possibility of parole, when the instruction does not also mention the Governor's power to commute a death sentence?","holding":"No. The Federal Constitution does not prohibit an instruction permitting a capital sentencing jury to consider the Governor's power to commute a life sentence without parole, and the instruction is not invalid because it omits the power to commute a death sentence. The States remain free to forbid such instructions as a matter of their own law.","reasoning":"The Court first found no adequate state ground barring review, since the state court rested its reversal on the Federal Constitution. It reviewed its capital cases and concluded that, beyond requirements such as individualized consideration and the Lockett rule on mitigating evidence, it has deferred to States on which substantive factors the sentencer may weigh. The instruction is not too speculative, because by raising the possibility of the defendant's return to society it focuses the jury on his probable future dangerousness, a factor approved in Jurek v. Texas. It does not undermine reliability as in Gardner v. Florida, because it gives accurate information known to both sides and leaves the defendant free to argue about commutation. It does not divert the jury from individualized sentencing, and Beck v. Alabama is distinguishable because a penalty decision has no single \"central issue\" like the guilt question. Informing jurors of the commutation power corrects a misleading label, since it is \"an accurate statement of a potential sentencing alternative.\" Adding an instruction on commuting death sentences would not restore neutrality and might lessen jurors' sense of responsibility, and California law forbade it anyway.","separate":"Justice Marshall dissented, joined by Justice Brennan, and by Justice Blackmun as to Parts II through V. He adhered to his view that the death penalty is always unconstitutional, and argued that the instruction misleads jurors into thinking death permanently removes the defendant, invites speculation about unknowable future clemency, injects a factor unrelated to the offense or the offender, and ignores the near-uniform view of other jurisdictions against such instructions. Justice Blackmun also wrote separately, saying the Court redefined the issue as future dangerousness and approved the instruction by \"intellectual sleight of hand.\" Justice Stevens dissented, arguing that the Court should not have granted certiorari, that the commutation power has nothing to do with culpability, and that the question is best left to state courts, and he joined the merits reasoning of Parts II to V of Justice Marshall's opinion.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Justice O'Connor wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Powell and Rehnquist (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep463/usrep463992/usrep463992.pdf"},"1988/87-1043":{"facts":"Ron Pair Enterprises, Inc. filed a Chapter 11 reorganization petition on May 1, 1984. The Government filed a timely prepetition claim of $52,277.93 for unpaid withholding and Social Security taxes, penalties and prepetition interest, perfected through a tax lien on the debtor's property. The debtor's reorganization plan provided for full payment of the claim but no postpetition interest. The Government objected, and the parties stipulated that the claim was oversecured.","procedure":"The Bankruptcy Court overruled the Government's objection, the District Court reversed, and the Sixth Circuit reversed the District Court, holding that Section 506(b) codified pre-Code law allowing postpetition interest on an oversecured claim only where the lien is consensual. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict with the Fourth Circuit.","question":"Does Section 506(b) of the Bankruptcy Code entitle the holder of a nonconsensual oversecured claim, here a tax lien, to postpetition interest?","holding":"Yes. Section 506(b) entitles a creditor with an oversecured claim to postpetition interest whether the lien is consensual or nonconsensual; only the fees, costs and charges component depends on an agreement.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text, holding that where the language is plain the inquiry should end there. Read naturally, and given the commas and the words \"and any\", the phrase \"interest on such claim\" stands apart from the later clause about fees, costs and charges provided for under the agreement, so interest is unqualified. A contrary reading would conflict with the rest of Section 506, which draws no consensual/nonconsensual line, and with Congress's use of the term \"security interest\" elsewhere when it meant to limit a provision to consensual liens. The Court found no policy reason or legislative history showing Congress meant to treat the two kinds of liens differently. Midlantic and Kelly v. Robinson were distinguished because they involved more ambiguous language and a clash with important state or federal interests, whereas here the text is clear and no such conflict exists. Finally, the pre-Code denial of interest on nonconsensual liens was an exception to an exception, recognized by few courts, confined to tax-lien cases, and only a guide to equitable discretion, so Congress would not have needed to repudiate it expressly.","separate":"Justice O'Connor dissented, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall and Stevens. She argued that the statute is not clear, because punctuation is not controlling and without the comma the \"provided for under the agreement\" clause would qualify interest too. She also argued that under Midlantic courts should not infer a change from established pre-Code practice absent some sign Congress intended one, that the pre-Code refusal of postpetition interest on nonconsensual liens was well established, and that the legislative history of Section 506(b) shows no intent to change it.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Blackmun delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Rehnquist and Justices White, Scalia and Kennedy (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep489/usrep489235/usrep489235.pdf"},"1980/79-5269":{"facts":"Edwards was arrested on January 19, 1976 on charges including robbery, burglary, and first-degree murder, and was given Miranda warnings. After questioning, he said he wanted an attorney before making a deal, and questioning stopped. The next morning two detectives came to the jail, and Edwards told the detention officer he did not want to talk, but the guard told him he had to. The detectives gave him Miranda warnings again, and after listening to part of an accomplice's taped statement, Edwards made incriminating statements.","procedure":"The trial court denied Edwards' motion to suppress, finding his statement voluntary, and he was convicted. The Arizona Supreme Court held that he had invoked his rights on January 19 but had waived them on January 20 by voluntarily giving his statement. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a suspect who has invoked his right to counsel during custodial interrogation be found to have waived it by responding to police-initiated questioning the next day after being advised of his rights again?","holding":"No. When an accused has invoked his right to counsel, a valid waiver cannot be shown merely by his responding to further police-initiated interrogation, even if he was advised of his rights. He may not be interrogated further until counsel is made available, unless he himself initiates further communication, exchanges, or conversations with the police.","reasoning":"The Court first held that the Arizona courts applied the wrong standard: waiver of counsel must be a knowing and intelligent relinquishment of a known right, not merely voluntary, and the state courts focused on voluntariness. Second, it held that additional safeguards apply once counsel is requested. Miranda said that \"the interrogation must cease until an attorney is present,\" and later cases did not abandon that view. Reinterrogation at police instance is inconsistent with Miranda. The Court noted that Edwards could have reopened the dialogue himself, and that voluntary statements he initiated could be used. Here the January 20 interrogation was at the authorities' instance, so his statement without counsel was not a valid waiver and was inadmissible.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in the judgment, rejecting a special waiver rule and applying the traditional Johnson v. Zerbst waiver standard; he found error because the guard told Edwards he had to talk. Justice Powell, joined by Justice Rehnquist, concurred in the result but did not join the opinion, finding its emphasis on who initiated contact unclear and objecting to any per se initiation rule; he would treat waiver as a question of fact under all the circumstances.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White wrote for the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall, Blackmun, and Stevens; Chief Justice Burger concurred in the judgment, and Justice Powell, joined by Justice Rehnquist, concurred in the result.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep451/usrep451477/usrep451477.pdf"},"1981/81-750":{"facts":"The Federal Home Loan Bank Board, acting under the Home Owners' Loan Act of 1933, issued a regulation saying a federal savings and loan association may include in its loan contracts a due-on-sale clause, letting it accelerate the loan if the secured property is transferred without its consent. The Board's preamble said federal associations would not be bound by conflicting state law. Appellees bought California property from borrowers who had given Fidelity Federal deeds of trust containing such clauses. Fidelity accelerated the loans after learning of the sales and began nonjudicial foreclosure, and appellees sued, relying on California's Wellenkamp rule that a lender may enforce such a clause only if the transfer impairs its security.","procedure":"The California Superior Court consolidated the suits and granted summary judgment for Fidelity, reasoning that the federal government had occupied the field. The California Court of Appeal reversed, holding that Wellenkamp controlled and that federal law had not pre-empted state due-on-sale law. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Does the Federal Home Loan Bank Board's due-on-sale regulation pre-empt California's Wellenkamp limits on the enforcement of due-on-sale clauses by federal savings and loan associations, and did the Board act within its statutory authority in issuing it?","holding":"Yes. The Board's regulation, which was meant to pre-empt conflicting state limits and was within the authority Congress gave the Board under the Act, bars application of the Wellenkamp rule to federal savings and loan associations. Federal regulations have no less pre-emptive effect than federal statutes.","reasoning":"The Court said ordinary pre-emption principles apply even though real property law is of special state concern, and that a conflict exists when state law stands as an obstacle to a federal objective. Where an agency is empowered to regulate, the questions are whether it meant to pre-empt and whether it acted within its delegated authority, not whether Congress itself spoke to pre-emption. The regulation and its preamble plainly showed the Board's intent to displace conflicting state law. The Court found a conflict even though the regulation only permits, and does not require, use of the clause, because Wellenkamp takes away the option and flexibility the Board gave associations and creates \"an obstacle to the accomplishment and execution of the full purposes and objectives\" of the regulation. The regulation's reference to the loan contract and the deeds' choice-of-law clause did not make state law control, since federal law is also part of the law of each State. Section 5(a) of the Act, its structure and its legislative history showed that Congress gave the Board broad authority over the operation and lending practices of federal associations, and the Board reasonably concluded that due-on-sale clauses protect their financial soundness. The Court did not decide whether the Act occupies the field.","separate":"Justice O'Connor concurred, writing that the Board's power to pre-empt state law is not limitless and that nothing in the opinion should be read to let the Board displace laws such as tax statutes and zoning ordinances not directly related to savings and loan practices. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice Stevens, dissented. He agreed the Board has broad power to regulate federal associations' lending practices but concluded Congress had not authorized the Board to make due-on-sale clauses enforceable as a matter of federal law, an \"extraordinary result\" intruding on state property and contract law.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, White, Marshall and O'Connor. Justice Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep458/usrep458141/usrep458141.pdf"},"1979/79-703":{"facts":"An Illinois statute made it unlawful to picket before or about a residence or dwelling but did not prohibit the peaceful picketing of a place of employment involved in a labor dispute (among other exceptions). Several appellees, members of the Committee Against Racism, peacefully demonstrated on the sidewalk in front of the home of the Mayor of Chicago to protest his alleged failure to support busing for school integration. They were arrested, charged under the statute, pleaded guilty, and received sentences of supervision.","procedure":"Appellees then sued in federal district court for a declaration that the statute was unconstitutional on its face and as applied, and for an injunction against enforcement. The District Court denied all relief. The Seventh Circuit reversed, finding the statute indistinguishable from the ordinance struck down in Police Department of Chicago v. Mosley and holding that it violated the Equal Protection Clause. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Does an Illinois statute that bans residential picketing but exempts peaceful picketing of a place of employment involved in a labor dispute violate the Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment by distinguishing between labor and nonlabor picketing?","holding":"Yes. The statute draws a content-based line between labor and other peaceful picketing, and neither the State's interest in residential privacy, its interest in protecting labor protest, nor a combination of the two justifies the distinction. The Court did not decide whether a uniform ban on all residential picketing would be valid.","reasoning":"The Court found the statute constitutionally indistinguishable from the ordinance in Mosley: picketing on public streets and sidewalks is expressive conduct within the First Amendment's preserve, and the exemption makes the permissibility of picketing depend on the message. When government discriminates among speech in a public forum, the Equal Protection Clause requires that the legislation be \"finely tailored to serve substantial state interests\". Residential privacy, though important, cannot save the statute because it is both over- and underinclusive and because nothing in the labor/nonlabor distinction bears on privacy. The interest in protecting labor protest presupposes that labor picketing deserves more First Amendment protection than public-issue picketing, which the Court rejected. Nor could the two interests combined, or a theory that a home used as a workplace waives privacy, justify the exemption, since the theory proves too little. The Court added that residential picketing remains subject to uniform, content-neutral time, place, and manner regulation.","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred, saying that although the Court relies on equal protection, what is really at stake is the First Amendment principle that government may not discriminate in regulating expression on the basis of content. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Blackmun, dissented: he argued that the Court mischaracterized the statute, which turns mainly on the character of the residence and permits picketing at homes used as places of business or employment; that it is a valid time, place, and manner regulation protecting residential privacy; and that appellees lacked standing to rely on hypothetical applications and were not themselves denied equal protection.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, Powell, and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447455/usrep447455.pdf"},"1980/79-1127":{"facts":"Ernest Smith was indicted in Texas for murder arising from an armed robbery in which his accomplice shot a clerk, and the State announced it would seek the death penalty. The trial judge informally ordered a psychiatric examination by Dr. Grigson to determine Smith's competency to stand trial, and Dr. Grigson interviewed Smith in jail for about 90 minutes and reported him competent. Smith was convicted. At the separate penalty phase, the State called Dr. Grigson, whose name was not on its witness list, and he testified from the interview that Smith was a severe sociopath who would continue to commit violent acts. The jury answered the Texas special questions against Smith, making a death sentence mandatory.","procedure":"The Texas Court of Criminal Appeals affirmed the conviction and sentence, and state habeas relief was denied. The federal District Court vacated the death sentence because of the admission of Dr. Grigson's testimony, and the Fifth Circuit affirmed, holding under the Fifth and Sixth Amendments that Texas may not use such psychiatric evidence unless the defendant was warned beforehand and assisted by counsel. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the State's use at a capital sentencing proceeding of a psychiatrist's testimony about future dangerousness, based on a pretrial competency examination conducted without Miranda-type warnings and without notice to defense counsel, violate the Fifth and Sixth Amendments?","holding":"Yes. The Fifth Amendment applies to the penalty phase, and a defendant who neither initiates a psychiatric evaluation nor offers psychiatric evidence may not be compelled to answer a psychiatrist whose findings can be used against him at capital sentencing unless he was warned of his rights and waived them. The Sixth Amendment was also violated because counsel was not notified that the examination would cover future dangerousness.","reasoning":"The Court rejected the State's argument that the privilege ends once guilt is adjudicated, noting that the privilege depends on the nature of the statement and the exposure it invites, and that death was a potential consequence of what Smith said; he could not be made the \"deluded instrument\" of his own execution. The testimony was not merely observational: Dr. Grigson relied on Smith's account of the crime, so the substance of his statements was used as evidence against him. The examination was neither a routine competency inquiry nor a sanity examination prompted by a defense of insanity, because Smith offered no psychiatric evidence. The reasons for Miranda warnings apply equally to a custodial psychiatric examination, and when Dr. Grigson testified for the prosecution his role became like that of a state agent, yet Smith was never warned. On the Sixth Amendment, the right to counsel had attached, the interview was a critical stage, and Smith lacked counsel's advice on the life-or-death decision whether to submit to it. The Court noted that the State may still prove future dangerousness in other ways.","separate":"Justice Brennan filed a concurring statement joining the opinion and adhering to his view that the death penalty is always unconstitutional. Justice Marshall joined all but Part II-C, for the same reason. Justice Stewart, joined by Justice Powell, concurred in the judgment, reasoning that the examination without notice to counsel made the testimony inadmissible under the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments and would not reach other issues. Justice Rehnquist concurred in the judgment on the Massiah/Sixth Amendment ground only, doubting that Fifth Amendment rights were implicated and declining to extend Miranda to psychiatric examinations.","disposition":"Affirmed (the death sentence stands vacated; the State may conduct further proceedings consistent with the opinion). Chief Justice Burger wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, White, Blackmun, and Stevens, and by Justice Marshall except for Part II-C.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep451/usrep451454/usrep451454.pdf"},"1982/81-1244":{"facts":"Respondents sued on behalf of all persons involuntarily confined in the Forensic Unit of a Missouri state hospital, challenging the constitutionality of their treatment and conditions. After a three-week trial, the District Court found a constitutional right to minimally adequate treatment and found violations in five of six general areas (physical environment, individual treatment plans, least restrictive environment, visitation/telephone/mail, and seclusion and restraint), but found staffing levels adequate. Respondents' four attorneys then sought fees under 42 U.S.C. § 1988 for about 2,985 hours.","procedure":"The District Court held respondents were prevailing parties, refused to exclude hours spent on unsuccessful claims, and awarded $133,332.25 after trimming one attorney's hours by 30 percent and denying an enhancement. The Eighth Circuit affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on whether a partially prevailing plaintiff may recover fees for work on unsuccessful claims.","question":"How should a district court set a reasonable attorney's fee under 42 U.S.C. § 1988 when a plaintiff prevails on only some of his claims?","holding":"The extent of a plaintiff's success is a crucial factor in setting the fee. Hours spent on unsuccessful claims distinct from the successful ones should be excluded; where claims are related and the plaintiff won substantial relief, the fee should not be cut merely because some contentions failed; and where only limited success was obtained, the court should award only a fee reasonable in relation to the results.","reasoning":"The Court started from the lodestar: hours reasonably expended times a reasonable hourly rate, with counsel exercising billing judgment. The \"results obtained\" factor then matters especially for partial success, raising two questions: did the plaintiff fail on claims unrelated to the successful ones, and did the plaintiff achieve a level of success that makes the hours a satisfactory basis for the award. Unrelated unsuccessful claims are treated as if raised in separate suits, so no fee is awarded for them. Where claims share a common core of facts or related legal theories, the court should look at the overall relief in relation to the hours reasonably spent; with excellent results the fee is fully compensatory, because \"The result is what matters.\" With only partial or limited success, the full lodestar may be excessive, and the court may exclude specific hours or reduce the award, exercising discretion and explaining its reasons. Applying this, the District Court's effort was commendable and its refusal to apportion mechanically was not error, but it did not address what fee was reasonable in light of the level of success.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, stressing that a lawyer seeking fees from an adversary must provide detailed records of time and services. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Marshall, Blackmun, and Stevens, concurred in part and dissented in part: he agreed with the Court's statement of principles and that fees may be awarded for hours reasonably spent even without success on every claim, but would have added factors such as delay in payment and risk of nonrecovery, and argued that the District Court's award was within its discretion and adequately explained, so the judgment should have been affirmed rather than remanded.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded for further proceedings. Justice Powell wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Rehnquist, and O'Connor; four Justices joined Justice Brennan's opinion concurring in part and dissenting in part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep461/usrep461424/usrep461424.pdf"},"1982/81-1578":{"facts":"New York's Human Rights Law prohibits employment discrimination, including pregnancy discrimination in employee benefit plans, and its Disability Benefits Law requires employers to pay benefits to employees disabled by nonoccupational conditions, including pregnancy. Delta Air Lines and other airlines, Burroughs, and Metropolitan Life maintained welfare plans covered by ERISA that, before the federal Pregnancy Discrimination Act took effect, did not cover pregnancy-related disability. They brought declaratory judgment actions asserting that ERISA pre-empted the state laws, and § 514(a) of ERISA supersedes state laws that \"relate to any employee benefit plan\".","procedure":"The District Courts held the Human Rights Law pre-empted, at least for the period before the Pregnancy Discrimination Act, and dismissed the airlines' challenge to the Disability Benefits Law on the view that ERISA § 4(b)(3) exempted plan provisions maintained to comply with it. The Second Circuit affirmed as to the Human Rights Law, but held § 4(b)(3) exempts only a plan that as an integral unit is maintained solely to comply with the disability law, and remanded. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Are New York's Human Rights Law and Disability Benefits Law pre-empted by ERISA § 514(a) as laws that relate to employee benefit plans, and does any exception, such as § 514(d) or § 4(b)(3), save them?","holding":"Both laws \"relate to\" employee benefit plans within § 514(a). The Human Rights Law is pre-empted as to ERISA plans only insofar as it prohibits practices that are lawful under federal law. The Disability Benefits Law is not pre-empted, although New York may not enforce it by regulating the ERISA plan itself, and may instead require a separate plan.","reasoning":"The Court read \"relate to\" in its normal broad sense, covering a law that has a connection with or reference to a plan, and found support in the rest of § 514 and in the Conference Committee's rejection of a narrower clause in favor of broad pre-emption. Section 514(d), which bars construing ERISA to impair federal law, saves the Human Rights Law to the extent it provides a means of enforcing Title VII, because Title VII relies on state fair employment laws and agencies and full pre-emption would disrupt joint enforcement. But Title VII does not depend on state laws for practices it does not prohibit, so pre-emption of state prohibitions of lawful practices does not impair it, and § 514(d) should not be expanded into a general saving clause given ERISA's all-inclusive pre-emption and narrow listed exceptions. On the Disability Benefits Law, § 4(b)(3) exempts \"plans,\" not portions of plans, and the word \"solely\" shows the whole plan must exist to comply with the disability law, so the test is whether the plan as an administrative unit provides only state-required benefits. The State may nonetheless require a separately administered compliant plan, so the law remains enforceable.","disposition":"Judgments affirmed in part, vacated in part, and remanded. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep463/usrep463085/usrep463085.pdf"},"1979/78-1779":{"facts":"Owen was appointed Independence, Missouri's Chief of Police in 1967. In 1972 the City Manager, Alberg, investigated the police property room and, although the City Counselor found no evidence of crime, asked Owen to resign and said he would be fired if he refused. At a council meeting on April 17 Councilman Roberts made accusations about the police department and moved that the investigative reports be released to the media and given to the prosecutor, and that the City Manager take appropriate action; the council passed the motion. Alberg discharged Owen the next day with only a notice citing the city charter, and ignored Owen's requests for charges and a hearing. The press tied the dismissal to the council's action, and a grand jury later returned no true bill.","procedure":"Owen sued the city, the City Manager and the council members in their official capacities under 42 U.S.C. § 1983 for lack of notice and a hearing. After a bench trial the District Court ruled for the defendants, and also found the city had a good-faith immunity. The Eighth Circuit first reversed, and after remand in light of Monell it again held that the city violated Owen's liberty interest under the Fourteenth Amendment but held that all respondents, including the city, were entitled to qualified immunity because they acted in good faith. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a municipality sued under § 1983 for a constitutional violation assert the good faith of its officers as a qualified immunity defense to liability?","holding":"No. A municipality has no immunity from liability under § 1983 flowing from its constitutional violations and may not assert the good faith of its officers or agents as a defense to liability.","reasoning":"The Court began with the text of § 1983, which \"creates a species of tort liability that on its face admits of no immunities.\" and with Monell's holding that municipalities are \"persons.\" Official immunities have been read into the statute only where they were well established at common law in 1871 and compatible with its purposes, but there is no tradition of immunity for municipal corporations and no mention of good-faith municipal immunity in the 1871 debates. The common-law doctrines that did protect cities do not help: governmental-function immunity rested on sovereign immunity, which a statute subjecting cities to suit abrogates, and discretionary-function immunity protected policy choices, but a city has no discretion to violate the Constitution. The statute's purposes of compensation and deterrence point the same way, since a good-faith defense would leave many victims without a remedy given officials' own qualified immunity. The two rationales for official immunity, unfairness to individuals and fear of chilling their decisions, are far weaker when the loss falls on the public treasury, and it is fairer to spread the cost among taxpayers than to leave it on the injured person.","separate":"Justice Powell dissented, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart and Rehnquist. He argued that Owen suffered no stigma and thus no constitutional deprivation, that the city could not be liable for a single councilman's statements, that the ruling imposes strict liability and punishes the city for failing to predict Roth, which came after the firing, and that the common law, the debates over the Sherman amendment, and the law of most States support a qualified good-faith immunity for local governments.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices White, Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens, in a 5-4 decision.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep445/usrep445622/usrep445622.pdf"},"1981/80-1874":{"facts":"Georgia Patsy alleged that her employer, Florida International University, had rejected her for more than 13 positions even though she was well qualified and had excellent evaluations, and that it filled positions through intentional discrimination on the basis of race and sex. She sued under 42 U.S.C. § 1983 for declaratory and injunctive relief or, alternatively, damages. Because the case came up on a motion to dismiss, the Court accepted her allegations as true.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the suit against the Board of Regents because she had not exhausted available state administrative remedies. A Fifth Circuit panel reversed, but the en banc court vacated that decision, held that a § 1983 plaintiff may be required to exhaust adequate and appropriate administrative remedies if specified conditions are met, and remanded for the District Court to decide whether exhaustion was appropriate. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Must a plaintiff exhaust state administrative remedies before bringing an action under 42 U.S.C. § 1983?","holding":"No. Exhaustion of state administrative remedies is not a prerequisite to an action under § 1983, and the Court declined to overrule its earlier decisions to that effect.","reasoning":"The Court noted that since McNeese in 1963 it had repeatedly and categorically rejected exhaustion in § 1983 cases, so the question was not one of first impression. In deciding whether to reconsider those decisions, it looked to congressional intent. The debates over the 1871 Act showed that Congress gave federal courts a paramount role in protecting constitutional rights, distrusted state authorities and fact-finding, and contemplated concurrent state and federal forums, though the Court cautioned that this history alone was not conclusive. Congress's recent enactment of 42 U.S.C. § 1997e, which created a narrow and detailed exhaustion requirement only for adult prisoners, showed that Congress understood exhaustion was not generally required and chose limited exceptions. A judicially imposed exhaustion rule would conflict with that scheme. The policy arguments for exhaustion cannot override congressional intent, are disputed, and raise difficult design questions better suited to legislation, so the Court concluded \"exhaustion of state administrative remedies should not be required as a prerequisite to bringing an action pursuant to § 1983\". A footnote left the Board's possible Eleventh Amendment defense open for the District Court on remand.","separate":"Justice O'Connor, joined by Justice Rehnquist, concurred, saying that policy favors an exhaustion requirement and that Congress might reconsider, but that precedent leaves the Court no choice; she concurred reluctantly. Justice White concurred in part, joining all but Part III-B, relying on stare decisis and the 1871 history, and arguing that the 1997e history does not show approval of a no-exhaustion rule and that exhaustion is generally a matter of judicial administration. Justice Powell dissented, joined by Chief Justice Burger as to Part II. He argued that the Eleventh Amendment and sovereign immunity are jurisdictional and bar the suit against the Board and that the Court should have addressed them, and in Part II that he would adopt the Fifth Circuit's flexible exhaustion rule.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Marshall delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Blackmun, Rehnquist, Stevens and O'Connor, and by Justice White except as to Part III-B.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep457/usrep457496/usrep457496.pdf"},"1984/84-28":{"facts":"A Washington statute, effective April 1, 1982, declares a \"moral nuisance\" any place where lewd films are regularly exhibited or where lewd publications are a principal part of the stock in trade. It defines lewd or obscene matter in terms tracking the Miller test, including that it appeals to the prurient interest, and defines \"prurient\" as that which incites lasciviousness or lust. Four days after the law took effect, various individuals and corporations that sell sexually oriented books and movies sued in federal court, claiming that the definition reached material arousing only a normal interest in sex and was overbroad on its face.","procedure":"The District Court rejected the constitutional challenges. A divided Ninth Circuit reversed and invalidated the entire statute on its face, holding that the word \"lust\" made the definition of prurience reach protected material and that the statute could not be given a limiting construction. State and county officials appealed, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Did the Ninth Circuit err by invalidating the entire Washington obscenity statute on its face because its definition of \"prurient\" included \"lust\", rather than invalidating it only to the extent it reached protected material?","holding":"Yes. Even accepting the Court of Appeals' reading of \"lust\", the ordinary rule of partial rather than facial invalidation applies, so the statute should have been invalidated only insofar as it reaches material appealing to normal sexual interest, and the remainder stays in force.","reasoning":"The Court assumed without deciding that the Court of Appeals correctly read the statute, and agreed that Roth did not treat material provoking only normal sexual responses as obscene. It then stressed the rules that courts should not decide constitutional questions in advance of necessity or formulate rules broader than the facts require, and that a statute may be partly valid and partly invalid. Those rules apply in First Amendment cases too, as Cantwell, Button and Grace show. Overbreadth doctrine is meant to protect people not before the court, but here the challengers are themselves publishers of the allegedly protected material, so the \"normal rule that partial, rather than facial, invalidation is the required course\" governs. If the statute is invalid only to the extent \"lust\" covers normal interest in sex, it would still validly reach the range of obscene publications, and the court could have struck the word since \"lasciviousness\" remained. The statute has a severability clause and Washington law favors severance, so the remainder keeps its effect.","separate":"Justice O'Connor, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, concurred, joining the opinion but stating that the federal courts should have abstained under Pullman and let Washington courts construe the never-enforced statute first. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented, adhering to his Paris Adult Theatre dissent that the statute is unconstitutionally overbroad on its face, and would have affirmed.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Blackmun, Rehnquist, Stevens and O'Connor. Justice Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep472/usrep472491/usrep472491.pdf"},"1982/80-2146":{"facts":"On January 3, 1978, two plainclothes Dade County detectives at Miami International Airport believed Royer fit the “drug courier profile” after seeing him buy a one-way ticket to New York in cash and check two suitcases tagged with the name “Holt.” They approached him, identified themselves as police, and asked for his ticket and driver's license, which showed his real name, Royer. After he gave an explanation for the discrepancy and grew more nervous, they told him they were narcotics investigators who suspected him of transporting narcotics. Keeping his ticket and license, they asked him to come to a small room about 40 feet away, and one detective retrieved his luggage without his consent. Royer unlocked one suitcase, in which marihuana was found, and did not object to the second being pried open; he was then arrested, about 15 minutes after the initial approach.","procedure":"The trial court denied Royer's motion to suppress, finding his consent voluntary, and he pleaded nolo contendere, reserving the suppression issue, and was convicted of felony possession of marihuana. The Florida District Court of Appeal, sitting en banc, reversed, holding he had been confined without probable cause beyond the limits of Terry v. Ohio, so his consent was tainted. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Was Royer illegally detained when he consented to the search of his luggage, so that his consent was tainted and ineffective to justify the search?","holding":"Yes. The plurality held that Royer was effectively seized and that, by the time he consented, the detention exceeded what is permissible on reasonable suspicion without probable cause, which did not exist. His consent was therefore tainted by the illegal detention and could not justify the search.","reasoning":"The plurality noted that the State bears the burden of showing voluntary consent, that police may approach people and ask questions without a seizure, and that Terry and its progeny created only limited exceptions to the probable-cause requirement for seizures, which must be temporary and last no longer than necessary. It agreed the detectives had reasonable suspicion to detain Royer briefly, but held that when they identified themselves as narcotics agents, told him he was suspected, and asked him to go to the police room while retaining his ticket and license, he was “effectively seized for the purposes of the Fourth Amendment.” Taking him to a small room, with his ticket, license, and luggage in their hands and no word that he was free to go, escalated the encounter so that, as a practical matter, Royer was under arrest. The plurality added that the conduct was more intrusive than needed, citing the availability of returning his papers, the lack of a safety reason for moving him, and the possibility of less intrusive means such as a trained dog sniff, and stated that investigative methods should be “the least intrusive means reasonably available”. It also agreed with the state court that probable cause to arrest was lacking, rejecting the argument that every nervous young man paying cash under an assumed name could be arrested.","separate":"Justice Powell concurred, joining the plurality, emphasizing the public interest in stopping drug trafficking but agreeing that Royer, in a small windowless room with his luggage seized, was under arrest and his consent was not voluntary. Justice Brennan concurred in the result: he would rest only on the holding that the seizure matured into an arrest without probable cause, considered much of the plurality's discussion of Terry stops unnecessary, and would have held that Royer was seized from the moment the officers took his ticket and license, without reasonable suspicion. Justice Blackmun dissented, arguing that the conduct was a minimally intrusive stop supportable on reasonable suspicion, that Royer voluntarily accompanied the officers and consented, and that the plurality failed to balance intrusion against law enforcement interests. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice O'Connor, dissented, arguing that every step the officers took was reasonable, that the suspicion conceded by the plurality justified the length and nature of the detention, rejecting the “least intrusive means” requirement as unsupported, and that Royer's consent to go to the room and to the search was voluntary.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White announced the judgment and wrote a plurality opinion joined by Justices Marshall, Powell, and Stevens; Justice Powell filed a concurrence and Justice Brennan concurred in the result, so the judgment had five votes. Justices Blackmun, Rehnquist, Chief Justice Burger, and Justice O'Connor dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep460/usrep460491/usrep460491.pdf"},"1983/83-226":{"facts":"An Arizona jury convicted Rumsey of armed robbery and first degree murder. Under Arizona's capital sentencing scheme the trial judge, without a jury, held a separate sentencing hearing in which the State argued three statutory aggravating circumstances, including that the murder was committed for pecuniary gain. The judge interpreted the pecuniary gain circumstance as applying only to contract killings, found no aggravating or mitigating circumstances, and sentenced Rumsey to life imprisonment without parole for 25 years for murder, plus 21 years for armed robbery, to run consecutively.","procedure":"On Rumsey's appeal and the State's cross-appeal, the Arizona Supreme Court held that the trial court misread the pecuniary gain provision, set aside the life sentence, and remanded for redetermination of aggravating and mitigating circumstances and resentencing. On remand the trial court found the pecuniary gain circumstance and sentenced Rumsey to death. In his mandatory appeal, the Arizona Supreme Court held under Bullington v. Missouri that the death sentence violated the Double Jeopardy Clause and reduced it to life imprisonment. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Double Jeopardy Clause bar Arizona from sentencing Rumsey to death after his first sentencing hearing resulted in a life sentence that was set aside on appeal because of a legal error?","holding":"Yes. Under Bullington v. Missouri, the first life sentence was an acquittal of the death penalty, and Arizona may not sentence Rumsey to death. The fact that the acquittal rested on a legal error does not change its double jeopardy effect.","reasoning":"The Court held that Arizona's capital sentencing proceeding has the features that made Missouri's resemble a trial in Bullington: the sentencer must choose between death and life imprisonment, is guided by detailed statutory standards, hears evidence and argument, and the State must prove aggravating circumstances beyond a reasonable doubt. That the sentencer is a judge rather than a jury does not matter, and the availability of appellate review does not make the appeal part of one continuing sentencing proceeding. The trial court's findings denying each aggravating circumstance, entered as a judgment for Rumsey on the issue of death, amounted to “an acquittal on the merits”, and an acquittal bars retrial even if it rests on legal error. United States v. Wilson, which allowed a government appeal from a post-verdict judgment of acquittal, was distinguished because there the guilty verdict could simply be reinstated, whereas here there was only one decisionmaker and no finding adverse to Rumsey to reinstate. The Court also declined the State's invitation to overrule Bullington, saying that “any departure from the doctrine of stare decisis demands special justification.”","separate":"Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice White, dissented. He continued to believe Bullington was wrongly decided, and argued that its implied-acquittal reasoning does not apply to a remand to correct a legal error, because the judge's discretion was confined, the record showed the State proved the aggravating circumstance, and under United States v. Wilson an error of law could be corrected without a second trial before a second factfinder. He would have reversed.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice O'Connor delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell, and Stevens (7-2); Justices Rehnquist and White dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep467/usrep467203/usrep467203.pdf"},"1980/79-1711":{"facts":"An organization of fishermen who harvest fish and shellfish off New York and New Jersey, and one member, sued various New York, New Jersey, and federal governmental entities and officials. They alleged that sewage, sludge, and other waste discharged into New York Harbor and the Hudson River and dumped directly into the ocean polluted the Atlantic and damaged their fishing grounds. They relied on many legal theories, including the Federal Water Pollution Control Act (FWPCA), the Marine Protection, Research, and Sanctuaries Act (MPRSA), and federal common-law nuisance, and sought injunctive and declaratory relief plus compensatory and punitive damages.","procedure":"The District Court granted summary judgment to the defendants, holding that private parties could not bring a federal common-law nuisance claim and that respondents could not proceed under the FWPCA and MPRSA without complying with the citizen-suit notice requirements. The Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit reversed, finding an implied statutory right of action preserved by the Acts' saving clauses and allowing the common-law claims. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on whether the Acts imply a private right of action, whether federal common-law nuisance is pre-empted, and whether a private citizen could sue for damages under federal common law.","question":"Do the FWPCA and MPRSA imply a private right of action for damages beyond their express citizen-suit provisions, may suits under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 enforce them, and is federal common-law nuisance for ocean pollution pre-empted by the two Acts?","holding":"There is no implied private right of action under the FWPCA or MPRSA, and the Acts' express remedies also show that Congress intended to foreclose a section 1983 remedy for their violation. The federal common law of nuisance has been fully pre-empted in the area of water pollution by the FWPCA and, for ocean waters not covered by that Act, by the MPRSA.","reasoning":"The key inquiry is congressional intent, found first in the statutory language, particularly its enforcement provisions. Both Acts have unusually elaborate enforcement mechanisms, including government suits, penalties, judicial review, and express citizen suits limited to injunctive relief after notice, so the Court concluded that Congress provided precisely the remedies it considered appropriate. The Court rejected the Court of Appeals' reliance on the saving clauses as ambiguous, doubting that \"any statute\" includes the very statute containing the clause, and found no basis for treating injured plaintiffs differently from citizen-suit plaintiffs, since citizens are those with an interest that is or may be adversely affected. Legislative history, including the Clean Air Act debates on which the citizen-suit provision was modeled, supported the view that damages were not contemplated. For section 1983, the comprehensive remedial schemes showed Congress meant to supplant it, and the saving clauses do not preserve a suit for violation of the statutes themselves. Because Milwaukee v. Illinois held the FWPCA pre-empts federal common-law nuisance, and the MPRSA scheme is equally comprehensive for ocean dumping, the common-law claims must be dismissed.","separate":"Justice Stevens, joined by Justice Blackmun, concurred in the judgment in part and dissented in part. He agreed that the statutes imply no private damages remedy (the conclusion of Part II-A), but through a different route: the statutes were enacted for the general public, not a special class. He dissented on section 1983 and pre-emption, arguing that the saving clauses and Senate Reports show Congress intended to preserve all existing remedies, including section 1983, and that the Court's reading deprives injured parties of effective federal remedies. He also criticized the Court's current approach to implied remedies as out of step with historical practice.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded (the judgment of the Court of Appeals was vacated as to the statutory and common-law claims). Justice Powell wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Marshall, and Rehnquist; Justices Stevens and Blackmun concurred in part and dissented in part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep453/usrep453001/usrep453001.pdf"},"1982/81-1863":{"facts":"Title 40 U.S.C. section 13k makes it unlawful to display in the Supreme Court building or grounds any flag, banner, or device designed or adapted to bring into public notice any party, organization, or movement, and the grounds are defined to include the public sidewalks around the building. Thaddeus Zywicki was told by Court police that he would be arrested if he continued to hand out leaflets on the sidewalk in front of the Court. Mary Grace was told she would be arrested if she kept displaying a sign bearing the text of the First Amendment on that sidewalk. Each left.","procedure":"Zywicki and Grace sued in federal district court for an injunction and a declaration that section 13k is unconstitutional on its face. The District Court dismissed for failure to exhaust administrative remedies. The Court of Appeals held the dismissal erroneous and struck down section 13k on its face as an unconstitutional restriction of First Amendment rights in a public place. The Government appealed to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does 40 U.S.C. section 13k, which bans displaying flags, banners, or devices on the Supreme Court grounds, violate the First Amendment as applied to the public sidewalks surrounding the Court building?","holding":"The statute covers appellees' conduct, so the constitutional question is reached. Section 13k is unconstitutional under the First Amendment as applied to the public sidewalks around the Court, because those sidewalks are public forums and a total ban on carrying signs, banners, or devices there is not sufficiently tied to the interests the statute serves.","reasoning":"The Court limited review to the display clause and to the sidewalks, and found that the picket sign and leaflets fell within the statute. Peaceful picketing and leafletting are protected expression, and public streets, sidewalks, and parks are traditional public forums where the government may impose only reasonable time, place, and manner regulations, and any absolute ban on a type of expression must be narrowly drawn to serve a compelling interest. The Court grounds as a whole are not automatically a public forum, but the perimeter sidewalks are indistinguishable from any other sidewalks in Washington, D. C., and Congress cannot destroy their public forum status by including them within a statutory definition of the grounds. The Court then examined the statute's purposes. A total ban on carrying signs on the sidewalks does not substantially serve the interest in protecting persons and property or maintaining order, since there was no suggestion that appellees obstructed access or caused any disturbance. Nor does it sufficiently serve the interest in avoiding the appearance that the Court is subject to outside influence, because the public would draw no different inference from a lone picketer on these sidewalks than from one across the street. The Court noted the sidewalks remain subject to reasonable time, place, and manner restrictions.","separate":"Justice Marshall concurred in part and dissented in part. He would have held section 13k unconstitutional on its face, as applied to the entire Court grounds and the public areas of the building, because it is a blanket prohibition not limited to expression that interferes with the administration of justice, and its continued existence chills expression. Justice Stevens also concurred in part and dissented in part. He would have construed the statute not to reach Zywicki's leaflets or Grace's sign, since neither was a display designed to bring a party, organization, or movement into public notice, and would have avoided the constitutional question while affirming the injunction against their arrest.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed to the extent indicated and otherwise vacated. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Blackmun, Powell, Rehnquist, and O'Connor; Justices Marshall and Stevens filed separate opinions concurring in part and dissenting in part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep461/usrep461171/usrep461171.pdf"},"1985/84-1279":{"facts":"Robert Van Arsdall was charged with the murder of Doris Epps, who was stabbed to death in an apartment in Smyrna, Delaware, after a New Year's Eve party; the State's case was circumstantial. Prosecution witness Robert Fleetwood testified that he saw Van Arsdall sitting on a sofa bed in the apartment shortly before the killing. Outside the jury's presence, Fleetwood acknowledged that a public drunkenness charge against him had been dropped in exchange for his promise to speak with the prosecutor about the murder, though he denied that this affected his testimony. The trial court barred all cross-examination before the jury about that agreement, citing Delaware Rule of Evidence 403.","procedure":"Van Arsdall was convicted of first-degree murder and a weapons offense. The Delaware Supreme Court reversed, holding that the ruling violated the Confrontation Clause and that a blanket prohibition on exploring bias is per se error requiring reversal without examining prejudice. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the trial court violate the Confrontation Clause by barring all cross-examination of a prosecution witness about the dismissal of a criminal charge, and if so, is such an error subject to harmless-error analysis rather than automatic reversal?","holding":"The trial court's refusal to allow any inquiry into the witness's possible bias violated the Confrontation Clause. Such an error, like other Confrontation Clause errors, is subject to Chapman harmless-error analysis, so the Delaware Supreme Court should have considered whether the error was harmless beyond a reasonable doubt.","reasoning":"The Court first rejected the argument that the state ruling rested on an adequate and independent state ground, since the opinion relied on federal law and lacked a plain statement of state grounds. On the merits, the Confrontation Clause guarantees an opportunity for effective cross-examination, and exposing a witness's motivation is a proper function of that right, although trial judges keep wide latitude to impose reasonable limits. Cutting off all questioning about an event that a jury might reasonably have found gave the witness a motive to favor the prosecution violated the Clause, and the defendant need not show outcome-determinative prejudice to establish the violation. As to remedy, the Court reasoned that the Constitution entitles a criminal defendant to a fair trial, not a perfect one, and that only a limited category of fundamental errors requires automatic reversal. Harrington v. California shows that the denial of an opportunity to cross-examine an adverse witness is not in that category, and Davis v. Alaska is not an automatic-reversal rule. The reviewing court should ask whether, assuming the damaging potential of the cross-examination were fully realized, it might nonetheless say the error was harmless beyond a reasonable doubt, considering the witness's importance, whether his testimony was cumulative, corroboration or contradiction, the extent of cross-examination otherwise allowed, and the overall strength of the prosecution's case.","separate":"Justice White concurred in the judgment, arguing that no Confrontation Clause violation occurs unless there is some likelihood the outcome was affected, so he would remand without holding that a constitutional violation occurred. Justice Marshall dissented from the harmless-error holding, arguing that a complete denial of cross-examination on bias should require reversal because an appellate court can rarely be sure it had no effect, and added that this Court cannot require state courts to apply harmless-error analysis to federal violations. Justice Stevens dissented on jurisdiction, arguing that the Court should not presume jurisdiction over an ambiguous state-court judgment and that the Delaware court's remedy may rest on state law, so he would dismiss the writ.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded to the Delaware Supreme Court. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Blackmun, Powell, and O'Connor; Justice White concurred in the judgment, and Justices Marshall and Stevens dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep475/usrep475673/usrep475673.pdf"},"1988/87-1207":{"facts":"Ray Will sued in Michigan state court under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, alleging that the Department of State Police and the Director of State Police, in his official capacity, denied him a promotion because his brother had been a student activist with a \"red squad\" file. The Civil Service Commission found the refusal was based on partisan considerations, and the state trial judge concluded Will had shown a constitutional violation and that both respondents were persons under § 1983.","procedure":"The Michigan Court of Appeals vacated the judgment against the Department, holding that a State is not a person under § 1983, and remanded on the Director's possible immunity. The Michigan Supreme Court agreed as to the State but also held that a state official sued in an official capacity is not a person. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a split among state and federal courts.","question":"Is a State, or a state official acting in his or her official capacity, a \"person\" who may be sued for damages under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, when the suit is brought in state court?","holding":"Neither a State nor its officials acting in their official capacities are \"persons\" under § 1983. An official-capacity suit is a suit against the office and so is no different from a suit against the State itself; official-capacity suits for prospective injunctive relief are treated differently.","reasoning":"Justice White reasoned that in common usage \"person\" does not include the sovereign, and that reading § 1983 to cover States would be \"a decidedly awkward way of expressing an intent to subject the States to liability.\" Congress must make its intent to alter the federal-state balance unmistakably clear, and the statute does not. Because a principal purpose of § 1983 was to provide a federal forum and the Eleventh Amendment bars suits against States there, Congress was unlikely to have meant to create a cause of action against States in state courts. Congress also did not override well-established common-law immunities such as sovereign immunity. The legislative history shows no intent to include States, and the Dictionary Act's reference to \"bodies politic and corporate\" does not supply a clear intent. Monell is consistent because municipalities, unlike States, are not protected by the Eleventh Amendment. Official-capacity suits are suits against the office and so against the State.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens, dissented. He argued that the Court relied on interpretive rules, especially the Eleventh Amendment clear-statement principle, that do not apply to a state-court suit, and that the Dictionary Act and the history of the 1871 Act show States are persons. He objected that the holding bars suits even where a State consents, and would remand to consider common-law sovereign immunity. Justice Stevens also dissented, arguing that official-capacity suits for prospective relief and awards of attorney's fees against States already treat the State as the real party in interest, so the State must be a person under § 1983, and that Eleventh Amendment fictions should not be imported into a state-court suit.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Rehnquist and Justices O'Connor, Scalia and Kennedy; four Justices dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep491/usrep491058/usrep491058.pdf"},"1979/78-1155":{"facts":"Under Nebraska law a prisoner found by a designated physician or psychologist to suffer from a mental disease or defect that could not be properly treated in prison could be transferred to a mental hospital. Jones, convicted of robbery, was placed in solitary confinement, set his mattress on fire and was badly burned. After treatment he was transferred to a state mental hospital under the statute. He intervened in a suit by other prisoners challenging the transfer procedures on procedural due process grounds. He was later returned to the prison, paroled on condition that he accept psychiatric treatment, and then reincarcerated after violating parole.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court declared the statute unconstitutional as applied to Jones and required notice, an adversary hearing before an independent decisionmaker, a written statement of reasons, and appointed counsel for indigent prisoners, and it enjoined transfer without them. The Supreme Court vacated for reconsideration of mootness, the District Court found the case not moot and reinstated its judgment, and the Supreme Court heard the appeal.","question":"Is the case moot, and does the Due Process Clause entitle a convicted prisoner to notice, an adversary hearing and other procedural protections, including counsel, before involuntary transfer to a state mental hospital?","holding":"The case is not moot, and the involuntary transfer of a prisoner to a mental hospital implicates a liberty interest protected by due process, requiring notice, an adversary hearing and the other procedures the district court specified. A majority agreed that qualified and independent assistance must be provided, but not that a licensed attorney is required.","reasoning":"The Court found a live controversy because Jones was still threatened with transfer and the injunction and declaration protected him. On the merits it identified two liberty interests. First, the statute and practice created an expectation that a prisoner would be transferred only on a finding of mental illness not treatable in prison, and state-created expectations bring due process protection that the State's own procedures cannot define away. Second, independent of the statute, a conviction does not authorize the State to classify a prisoner as mentally ill and subject him to involuntary psychiatric treatment; \"the stigmatizing consequences of a transfer to a mental hospital for involuntary psychiatric treatment,\" with mandatory behavior modification, are a deprivation of liberty. Weighing the strong State interest against the prisoner's interest and the risk of error, the Court held the medical nature of the decision does not dispense with an adversary hearing and approved the procedures required below. Four Justices would also require counsel for indigent prisoners.","separate":"Justice Powell concurred in part: he joined all but Part IV-B and agreed qualified and independent assistance must be provided, but said a licensed attorney is not required and a psychiatrist or other qualified mental health professional could serve, so the judgment was modified accordingly. Justice Stewart, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, dissented, arguing the case is moot and should be vacated with directions to dismiss. Justice Blackmun dissented separately, viewing the problem as one of ripeness, with no present case or controversy because officials had shown no present intent to transfer Jones again.","disposition":"Affirmed as modified to conform to Justice Powell's separate opinion on counsel. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, Powell and Stevens as to Parts I, II, III, IV-A and V; Part IV-B (counsel) was joined only by Justices Brennan, Marshall and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep445/usrep445480/usrep445480.pdf"}});